      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0007" n="art0007">
        <head>ILLUSTRATED FASHIONS.</head>
        <p>ILLUSTRATED FASHIONS.</p>
        <p>Hovering as we do between Easter and Whit- suntide our thoughts are naturally much occupied with the contents of stir -wardrobes, and the bright spring sunshine seems to per- meate the feminine mind with a desire to don as gay attire as is feasible, and the exceedingly alluring and fascinating goods displayed in the shop windows only add fuel to the fire of our desires. The old-fashioned plan was to replenish -the contents of one's wardrobe half-yearly, in spring and autumn respectively, with a supreme disregard of weather or temperature. This was simply an echo of the Spartan rule by which our grandmothers deprived themselves of the luxury of a fire at one particular date in spring, only to resume it in autumn, the customary "weather permitting" being viewed almost as sacrilege in their eyes. The younger and present genera- tion of womankind are undoubtedly less strong- minded than their progenitors, and, whether it</p>
        <p>be to their credit or not, comfort plays a more ' prominent part in the daily routine of their lives. Blouses are very pretty this season, and the pretty sample sketched is no exception to the rule. It is a delightfully soft intermingling of flowered pink muslin and lace.</p>
        <p>The hard linen collar has not yet returned to adorn the throats of the fashionable, and is still only beloved by. the mannish maiden. Nevertheless, there is something particularly</p>
        <p>.. ~r.r.i#:,-,ir . 1 eirfift.,..:l-54, e ~,,::).77*.-iii, t „ri.,,,-..._, .. 4, ......--.&gt; . - r - / :;\ , . :.,../ , , 4 , . 07, /. /ii ~ri y..): -,,,.., 7 'vs- 5/A• . --,i-3 ,ik,..1, ~, , ..,,,. ,l. 11 : 4: 11\,, \ 1,:,,,,,1i,,..- v 111 ,-,-- _ .. to: .ii , I, \ , 4, , ~ ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <head>‘A-inETTY BLOIISI.</head>
        <p>‘A-inETTY BLOIISI.</p>
        <p>attractive in the smoothness of its shining clean /surface which seem* to add rather than detract from a fresh wholesome complexion, and I fancy that Dame Fashion, with her usual fickleness, will again shortly smile thereon. Still it must, be confessed that after contrasting the comfort of a transparent lace collar with two-and-a half inches of starched surface, one can only wonder • and admire the . determination with which mankind braves out the hottest summer-day behind the stifling restrictions of a perfectly dressed linen collar. Truly -we must be the weaker sex. There are so many pretty ties and cravats this season, most of the cravats being nearly related to a stock. Some cf these are made in coloured lawn, and are about eight-inches in width. They are wound round the throat, the ends being crossed behind and'brought bas* and pinned in front, the ends showing for the most part a lace edge. Others again are not se wide and are only passed once round the neck and crossed over in front, the edges being cut up into tabe edged with Valen. eiennes or solaw other fine lace. The smart</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>SKART COAT.</head>
        <p>A SKART COAT.</p>
        <p>coat illustrated is -exceedingly up to date as re cut, and opens over a dainty waistcoat. It 'a owing very much to the introduction of th. newest coats that these lovely waistcoats or vests have come into ‘Kcaninence, and many of them in rich creamy or tinted satins afford a splendid opportunity for the clever worker to display her cunning needlework, as expressed by wre the and, garlands of flowers worked in natural L:olours, interspersed in some instances with jewel work. Most ,of them are cut very much in the same fasliien as a man's waistcoat, showing an inner vest of ;fine lace and muslin. Other very charming samples are executed in suede embellished • with eyrays of flowers in ribbon work. These pre very soft looking and set off the figuru to advantage, as they cling closely.. Lace of Lill kinds appears in quantities on nearly every vest or front, and Trish guipure is 'much in• vogue. With the vests of rich old- faehioneddooking brocade lace cravats are always .worn, some of these being of such length that after lhey are passed- round the neck twice they are -knotted once or twice and pulled through the waistcoat to reappear at the waist. There is some- thing at once fascinating and very picturesque in these old new-fashioned vests which cannot fail to appeal to the artistic eye, especially when they are .worn, as is usually the case, in conjunction with a Louis X.Y. coat.</p>
        <p>The charming hat depicted is made( a mush- soom tinted satin straw bent and dented to suit the face, and trimmed with shaded pink roses. The .crown is surrounded by a soft swathing of creamy lace and pink silk intermingled, caught rather to one side of the centre back by a steel buckit. In regard to millinery it is impossible to say that any one shape is correct, for one can wear anything provided it is smart any becoming, two very different things, as some women fail altogether to realize. How to wear the</p>
        <p>:TIARMING HAT.</p>
        <p>, latest chapeaux is a problem which, although solved by our clever sisters across the channel, is at present much in the minds of the English girls. In order to successfully adjust a fashionable hat (which, of course, turns off the face) is to arrange it on the low dressed coiffure so that the hat is not back on the head but up from the head. The 'brim must not run with the line of the head, but up and away from it, allowing the line of the forehead and the hair to be seen. The brim may project well forward, but it projects at an -upward angle, and it is in this cunning adjustment that the success of the dainty Parisienne lies, so far as her headgear is concerned.</p>
        <p>COQUETTZ</p>
        <p>, 4.- • • e,„--6 . • :...--,,,...-, N. , •% „ , fr / '.., ,o. .. N .5.. ~... 0 . . 7—t- -- —--—_ _ _ ,-z.. A- IA t•&gt;- - V ' • lig.. ! ,-17 , ;'''. 14. 3.; ---"r•-.1,3, r . ...!.. I. , ./ 'i .1 \ 141111 if p . or .1.,1it riv , ' ...... w.... ~.„-i...... --711,......„ Alw . •-, rl r'7 6 , a w ti\\ o 1\k\...._1 1 •</p>
