      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <head>BLE,</head>
        <p>BLE,</p>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Coronation Service in Olio Abbey has been mere- I *fully shortened, but, as it is, it is divided into nineteen parts,•and it is difficult to see hew it can all be gone through in two or three hours The sing however de- sires to be back at Buckingham Palace by 2 o'clock and as the service does not commence till eleven,this will no dcubt be somehow managed. The Bible,which is to be presented to the wing, and upon which His Majesty will I take the oath, will be supplied by the Universities of I Oxford and Cambridge, and rot by the British and Foreign Bible Society, owia.e, to the fact that the Bib'e circulated by the Society dots not contain the Apecryphs. The ceesing of the theatres on the evening of Corona- tion Pay, and the following day, by order of the Lord Chamberlain, is in striking ceistrast with the precedeet established at the Coronation festivities of Queen Victoria On that occasion the theatres gave free performance:, among them Drury-lane, Covent Garden,the Haymarket, St James' and others, whieb are still in existence. The cost of opening the theatres was provided for in t,e Coronation expenses, and in 1338 about £3,000 was ex- pended for giving free theatrical performances. It is understoed that the closing of the theatres on the ferte- coming occasion will not make any difference financially to those empleyed at them, whilst their shutting up wi I add to the inducements to the general public to spend the Coronation holidays out of town.</p>
        <p>_ The opera eeasen will be a very brilliant one Ws Cea!, and there will be many " gala " nights, when the King and Queen will be present, Their Majeatiel are genuine levera of music, which is perhaps more than c he said °me many who frequent the Opera Roue. Tee Royal boxes take up a great deal of accommodat ' ion aed the mall amongst subscribers and others to secure places during the seams has been phanornimal. Many, hea- -1 ever, go for the social rather tlaatr the musical attrac- tions, and since it has become the fashion to go to the opera many lovers of music find themselves crowded out The Academy contains fewer pictures this year, as 1 they have been spread out rather mere in the hanging, and there are many large canvases which take up mush room. All this is no doubt, bard upon the artists whosa weeks hare been returned sagely for the want of room, but it is an improvement from the public point of view As a rule the general lover of pictures finds it impeisibla to do jutic,, to anythir* like the number exhibit's-1, even in three or four visits. But the ordinary visitor has an easy way of gettieg over this difficulty. He or she gees once or twice and stays for two or three hour,. To say at the end of such visits that the Academy this Year is "good" er " bad," does scant justice to thr exhibitore, but the reduced number of pictures at lea4t enables everyone to pay more attentioa to their indivi- dual merits.</p>
        <p>Their Majesties Second Court, and the Private View at the Royal Academy have afforded us proof pesitive that the styles orlB3o will be the predominating owe this season. These modes include the high sorealet belts which are unquestionably leading up to high waist', the old world fichrni, and pilerines.the deep berthes, well off the shoulders, and the big Leghorn hats, with lace " curtains" drooping over the brim and sweeping Paradise plumes. Then there is the tassel which pro- mtees semi to rival the claims of black velvet, as a trimnfne. Very beautiful indeed is the silken tassel of ' to-der, in its lovely colours of every hue. Needless te say the fabrics which cempesek the Princese ef Waiee' Court gown, were of home manu- facture, 'nal moreover the gown was built in Lendoe. Those privileged to see this exquisite " creation " will perchance have come to the conclusion that it is scarcely necessary to go to Perla for a succeesful toilette,and th.t patriotism would prove less of a loss, and more of a I rain, than they have hitherto imagined. But to resume. There is very little dauht that colours are much more becoming to the'Princess than bleck, and in the rich oyster ground satin, broesded with clusters of roses and ixia, in blended Wee of MitlIVP, pink, delicate real, and pale geld, Her Royal Highness looked particularly well. And all the stiffness in the Princess's manner—at one time not infrequently mistaken for coldness er indifference—has disappeared, her kindly graciousness reminding one rnere end more of her mother, the late Dechess of Track. tee reconciles us to any exaggeration of fashion, and possibly we shall soon beeome accustomed to the " jolly hag' sleeve, which has found on great favour emerg the smartest 970123e15. That so much accommodation should be wasted is a matter to deplere, in these days of ' pncketless skirts. One could pack all the Inge&amp; e necessary for a week-end visit into a pair of fashiosiable sleeves, and yet it is impossible to make the least use of all this space. Not a few women are mekine or havine made for them a little slit on the inner side of the " bag," through which they stuff a microscopic hand- kerchief The search for, and reecue of this little accessory, is often amusing' in the extreme, and, taken altogether, the idea would nct seem to he an altogether brilliant one.</p>
        <p>Straw embroidery, en-tulte and chiffon gowns, is the latest novelty in the way of trimmings. This Appears toe, on fichus and cravats, also on many of ths daintiest blouses. An embroidery or garniture made of straw sounds impossible. but the effect jugtifies the meatv,and it is a very soft variety of straw—which looks very lite chenille—that is utilised Fnr millinery purposes it tr been in considerable request of late, mixed with chiffon. acd it is now aderning (P) tbe inevitable "lace curtain" that falls over tbe brim, and</p>
        <p>Much has been said about keeping the Boer worsen and girls in the Refugee Camps, but one thing is cer- tain, that they will 2n back to their farms with an im- proved knowledge of howeholddntiee. There is hardlf a rtiral population in Europe not poeessed of some knowledge of spinning and knitting wet these we almost unknown arts amongst the Borr women. The Boers perhaps weferred to go without stockings, and is have their clothes unmended, but in n y case the ign,r- awls of the Boer womea in many of the elements,- y duties of the housewife teertainly agtonished our people. They have learned much in the Rafugee Camps, and it is the wmcral opinion that the social conditions ef the people will be greatly altered and improved by the war, Sir James Sawyer. the distioguisiled Bi rmingham physician recently offered some remarks en the subject of bee keeping, which merit attention. At a meeting of the Warwickshire Beekeepers' Associat,on, heFirn- mended very strongly, the use of honey as an article of feed, remarking that if its nutritive value were better known, the e,onatimption of honey would increase very largely. It was capable, he said, of ministering to the activity of body and mind, and by the expulsion of noxiotv materials from the hody,oontributed greatly to the maintenance of health and comfort. At the same time it may be added, care should he taken in purchas- ing honey to obtain only that produced at home. There Is a good deal of rubbish in tli market, imported fr)rri thread, te which Bir James Sawyer's remarks do•nst any means apply. A bright little hank, and one written with feeling', and a sense of humour, is " Miss Carmichael's Con- orients " (Pearson). Very lovable and winsome Mary Carmichael proves to be,and one finds oneself regretting the terrible retributicn—not infrequently or unfit- nervedly meted out to the ha:rimed flirt—that falls to her lot. There is a very goad moral to this story'</p>
        <p>MD GE.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>NOSE BY ANY 01 HER NAME.</head>
        <p>A NOSE BY ANY 01 HER NAME.</p>
        <p>lUnfortunately, in this chorus of congratulation one, false note has been struck. Smile anonymous friend has sent Bertha a copy, facetiously marked with red ink in: telling places, of a treatise on the "Art of Beauty." This treatise, though adapted to the needs of London/seems to have originated in New York: It opens with an address to the reader, sod gess on to deal in detail with I physical imperfections and the method of remedying \' them. Lt me quote some of the marked passages : DEAR fderuntE cat bra,—The object of this littl- blohlet is to direct the attention of the trader to the many and varied imperfections of the face with which we are apt to be endowed, and to call your attention t 3 the fact that there is in this vast Metropolis a plate? where relief may be found.</p>
        <p>THE E&amp;HS.</p>
        <p>If the ears are large, ili-shaped, or deformed in any way, they are soon changed to a perfected state. A I irregularities and deformities are paislessly,permanently and suectusfully corrected, thereby rendering the features symmetrical, harmonious, and expressive.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>NOSE.</head>
        <p>THE NOSE.</p>
        <p>As Shakespeare says, " A goad nese hi requisite." Na other feature of the face bears such a relation to what is known as beauty. and no other feature is as helpful or as fatal to man's socafort .</p>
        <p>Few persons can boast a perfect nose. T:fis being as, it will interest many to knew that this prominent feature of the face may be improved where it is imper- fect. Everybody is familiar with the various tpes of noses, and may see many of each during a stroll on any of our thoroughfares. Some are repugnant, other; pleasing, but all give some determining character, either sympathetic or forbidding, to the 'face. Many a countenance, otherwise admirable is ruined to the eye by the form of the nose, and it is often remarked that such or such a person would be " good- looking" were it notfor his or her nose. . Many are of the opinion Oat tho nose Nature has given, or the one that accident has deformed, is beyond remedy. This is an error, for, • when one reflects upon the fact that the nose is greatly compeaed of cartilage, It will be admitted that few things are easier than giv- ing It direction of form. If your nose is Roman and you wauld bevels Grecian, it can be changed to conform with your idea of shape. If pugged, it can be lowered ; if drooping, or hawk- billed, it can be given true angles; if crooked, it can be straightened; if depressed, it can be raised. In other words, if it does not please you, it can be remedied Si do so, nainlessly and quickly, by the most modern and scientific means known to speeialists.—,From " A Lon- doner's Log-Book," in the " Cornhill Odagazine" for May.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>irHE KING AND CONSUMPTION CURES.</head>
        <p>irHE KING AND CONSUMPTION CURES.</p>
        <p>Another Life Saved.</p>
        <p>Great interest le being taken not only hr the medical fraternity but by the King in the open-air treatment for Consumption. This will be particularly interes'ing to the working classes, whose death-rite is already high compared with the rich. Consumption chest, and lung mil:dab:its are chiefly responsible for the excessive mor- tality. &amp;tealso investigation proves what has often been suspected, namely, that the only real chance of stamp- ing out consumption is by getting thoss who are threat- seed by it to pay attention to chip blood supply, and thug relieve the wastage and danger to the lunge. The instances (new too numerous to be any longe• neglected by the ecienttfie men who are eng4ged on the question) in which worioubted nonsunitrion hAs teen cured by Dr. Williams' pink pills for pale people, demand that this treatment, which is of national importance, should be at least tried in all cases of threatened consumption or its forerunner. anaemia. One of the most interesting of recent curse is that of Mrs. Hornby, 25, MlLeed- street. Breckfleld-road, South Liverpool Ten years ago Mre Hornby was stricken with rheu- matic fever, which left her exceedingly weak and Her heart also 'Teemed to be affected-; a severe cough developed, and she became so feeble and breathless that the could nos walk many yards without pausing to rest. .Thres of the leading specialists in lung disease were consulted, but wii bout appreci ibis benefit "After leading an almitst hopeless existence;" Mrs anreby continued, " I began to feel that nothing would do me any gnod. The doctors prescribed medicine, bat without any marked improvement. My friends had frequently spoken to me of other people whe had been greatly tenefi•ecl or absolutely cured by taking Dr. Williams' pink pills for Pale peeple,and I &amp;torts/lined es a last resource to follow their advice. I procured a box of Dr. Williams' pink pills. At the end if tbe first I week after I began to take the pills the woman who ' had been doing, my house work remarked on the change in my appearance, end from that time I • trended.' I 'can now do my family washing and my ewe heusework. My cough has stopped, and also the epitting of blood. I can now walk miles with less difficulty than I could formerly walk yards, the shortneas in my breath having I disappeared, I have acquired DPW blood and renewed health ;in fact. I have never felt bitter." , It is impossible to question that Mrs Hornbv hie really been snatched from the imminent peril of a death from consumption The lesson is one which should be taken to heart by all consumptives, and indeed by all who suffer from any disease traceable to impoverished or vitiated bleed, anaemia, rheornat sm. skin eruption*, general weakness and the -like, all of which have been treated with success in the same manner.</p>
        <p>THE RISKS OF A BANK CLERK. ,</p>
        <p>" A Walk-elerk," siva a writer on Bank Life in the May " Windsor Mav:azioe," " rune the chance every morning,•of being robbed by gangs of skilled thieves, who make a study of him, and watch their opportunities ad sometimes succeed, though not often, for he studies them also. _ . _ _ . .</p>
        <p>Clerks engsgrd ita foreign and Colonial hanks have,of c'turse, their own special class of risk., front the climate and rtherwise. Those ia the Australian Bush, for instance, are exposed sometimes Co visits from bush- rangers, as the cash which must he kept is a great temptation to those scampi,. In one case a small branch was '13,1r1 up' while the macagsr was ia his bath, and some money was taken; but in others the clerks have succesafully defended the bank property at the risk of their lives. Here in England this is unnessary, though nct many years ego, when the rioters were breaking ,the club windows in Bt. James's-street. firearms were served out to many bank clerks, though happily occa- sion did not arise for their use.</p>
        <p>The risks in many banks (such a 3 those in the West Indies, the West coast of Africa, etc.) are of a different oature,the local rev rs being of so (timorous • character that it hal been found necessary to offer salaries much in excess of the usual rates in order to induce men to go there at all. Most banke give pensions to such of their clerks as, from ape or :11 health, are unfit for work, and in many the scale adopted is similar to that in the Civil Service, one-sixtieth of salary for every year of service up to forty years. At many banks there is a fixed scheme, but in some it is left to the directors' discretion. Schemes for providing for widows and orphans are also I* existence, and fairly fiourishiag. It should be men- tioned that most banks now require any applicant for a post to papa a modPrately stiff examination and to insure his life for a small ainount."—The '1 May Windsor."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>CORONATION BONFIRES.</head>
        <p>THE CORONATION BONFIRES.</p>
        <p>Bonfires are the signs of peace in these days. as once thE y were the signals of war. Success will surely attend the workof the Bonfire Committee formed at the Souse of Commons last month, which intends to cover all the heights of the country with joyful fires in honour of the Royal Coronation in June.ln Jubilee days nauchwas•done but the efforts of the past promise toll). exceeded.Enelan d and Walla hive been divided into three zones—Southern, Midland, and Northern—and local committees ere being formed In each to organize the celebration. At 10 pm on June nth every hill in England ehotsld burst into light, with the flare rf bonfires, and red, white, and blue lights, and lot an hour rockets should go up in sign of jubilation. In some places there will be double bonfires to horvmr the double co, confirm, and local enthusiasm wi I no doubt devise sptclal fermi of Illumination. From co ry bill rlso will rice the sound of the ."4. National Anthem," and the hellfire arrangements will carry the ontvratd sign of gladness to many places remote from the towns. In kcotlind the bonfires and rocktts are timed for about half-an-boor later, owing to the long light in the North. We way be sure that Ireland will not fail to join in the celebration. This is a form of the public manifestation of loyal re- joicing that will appeal to all of us, and Lord Cranborne and tie friends aro to be congratulated on the excellent work they have undertaken. They are encouraging local effort and not creatlng central funds. —Navy and 4,•)ny.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRI D Y</p>
        <p>EVENTS OF THE WEEK ILLUSTRATED.</p>
        <p>• The great American Shipping Trust, 'ate greate4 of all combines ever yet effected in its far-reaching complications, still continues to be the one absorb- ing subject of conversation, and more and more each Iday the feeling is spreading through the country that it is a most serious menace to British welfare ig general, regulating as it may do all Transatlantic commerce to suit American profits rather ,than British • interests. The Government, speaking through the mouth of 34. Gerald Balfour, President of the Board of Trade</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>;ME AND GOVERNMENT</head>
        <p>;ME AND GOVERNMENT</p>
        <p>APATHY:</p>
        <p>TEM RT. HON. GERALD BALFOUR, P.C., ILP., PREAIDZET OF THE BOARD OF TRADE.</p>
        <p>seem to have no ofacial cognisance of this enormously iniportant matter, and are coining in for sevel:e criticism an all sides, irrespective of party politics, which are merged in a national question of such vital importance. True a Select Committee has been appointed to inquire into the question of subsidies and how various lines stand legally with regard to these public payments, but this is generally felt to be but a comparatively small matter at it time when. vigorens action is undoubtedly called for. It is generally hoped th A the Government Will take matters ap officially and deal some counter-blow to this American bid for the supremacy of the Atlantic. The Cunard and the Canadian lines ars stla outside? the Trust and Will pre`sumably remain so. and. it is thought that they, if materially a.ssi-ted,,may be able to grapple even with such formidable opposi- tion. Meanwhile, Canadian trade and the interttts of that Colony's shipping and railroads are threat- ened with ultimate extermination at "the hands of their rivals, and the Canadian authorities seem fully alive to the menace. Undoubtedly the Shipping Trust is one of the most scrims problems which the British people have ever been called upon •to face, and our fight against it will be indicative of the true state of our national 4i:int)+,</p>
        <p>The happiest event in the history of Spain. for a long period will be celebrated next week whom King Alfonso XIII. will have completed his sixteenth year, when, according to the law of his country, he may -take up the reins of sovereignity in his own hands, with all its rights and privileges, and also its duties and heavy responsibilities. Whether' the aosumption of full sovereignty over Bpa.in at this juncture by a youth of seven- teen is a pros- pect to give un- mixectijoy to the young ulex him- self is not for us to say, but at all events it marks a new era, and, it is to be sincerely hopcd, a happier and a more pros- perous one, in Spanish history. His youth and the burden of his great position make King Alfonso one of the most inter- esting pensonali- ties of our time, whose future career inevitably forms a fascina- ting, 'subject for conjectureamong all who have a penchant fo r prophecy as to</p>
        <p>ALFONSO XIII. OF SPAIN</p>
        <p>WIIOSE COXING OF AGE WILL BE CELEBRATED NEXT</p>
        <p>the trend of in-</p>
        <p>ternaVonal politics. Already his youth has been much troubled with public cares and his health has been the cause of anxiety to his , well-wishers, but personal observers speak of his Majesty as exhibiting firmness and maturity of 'char- acter beyond his years, and there is a general hope that his reign may prove fruitful of beneficent ad- ministration and national reforms, and that his power and influence may do much towards amelior. ation of the internal discords of tee encient and noble kingdom. he is called to govern. The official c,elebmtion in honour of the coming of age of the Voting 'monarch will begin •on Thursday next, May 15th, and will last until the following Tuesday. The occasion will of course be marked with -a good deal of pomp and k, ceremony, but hardly as much rrnificence as one might expebt at the Spanish nrt. In view of our own Coronation next mouth the event will be regarded with especial interest i this country and the Colonies, although, of course, the ceremony will be totally different from that to be observed when King Edward VII. .and his gracious Consort are crowned.</p>
        <p>On Monday the Royal Academy threw open its portals to the public aid has this week been crowded with throne of eager visitors. The private view, which was largely attended, was held on Friday, and on Saturday evening the Prince of Wales was present at his first Academy Banquet, following the precedent of his father, who always put in an appearance when Heir-Apparent. Naturally this will be a very important year, and the competition for places has, therefore, been keener than ever. The result, however, is not in proportion, and this year's Academy is pronounced to be only a mediocre one, though, of course, it contains much work of strikinemerit. One picture</p>
