      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>ROYAL ACADEMY:</head>
        <p>THE ROYAL ACADEMY:</p>
        <p>UR EDWARD POTSTIA, TIIE PHIisIDENT, IN HIS STUDIO. by reason of its 'subject stands out pre-eminent in , interest in this. the Coronation season—the State portrait of His Majesty by Mr. Like Fildes, R.A., who, it is admitted upon all sides, has been very successful in a most difficult task, giving not only a good likene.ss of his august subject, but lending to the whole an air of regality and kingliness, which Is peculiarly !fitting. Of Qqeen Victoria there are wo processional pictures, of which by far the better is Mr. John Charlton's great canvas of the Diamond Jubilee procession passing St. James's Palace. Apart from Mr. Fildes' picture, this is essentially a portrait year, which is dominated by Mr. Sargent, who also carries all before him at the New Gallery. and proves himself the greatest of living painters. His Duchess of Portland is a fascinating piece of work, in Odition to which he szhibits :1746 Ladies Acheson, The Moses Hunter,.</p>
        <p>ME. C. HUTCHINGS WINNER OF THE AMATEUR GOLF CHAMPIONSHIP.</p>
        <p>Interest it evoked all over the country, and even non-golfers have been led to follow the play from day to day and to speculate upon the winner. It was throughout a meeting full of surprises, and the final was one of the most exciting in the history of the game. For the first time for ten years' two Englishmen Charles Hutchings, of the Royal Liverpool -Club, and Sidney H. Fry, of the Mid-Surrey Club—were the players, and victory went to the feigner. The match was a csse of the young one -the old generation of golfers, as Mr. Hutchings is fifty. three, and did not touch a club until he was over thirty, .whereas Mr. Fry is a much younger man, and considered one of the deadliest putters known. The concluding portion of the great match 'was followed by a crowd numbering about four thousand, and they are not likely to forget the superb exhibition in a hurry.</p>
        <p>Each season, as it comes round, brings to light some new musical lion, and it seems highly probable that .Mr. Arthur Hartmann, the newest of new violinists, will be the Celebrity of the year in Music. His first recitals have , been very great successes, and though very little preliminary flourish of triuraphts/heralded his debutiie was marked down immediately as a great musician, brilliant in execution and rich in tone, and an</p>
        <p>artist who had "arrived" at his first attempt. He is a Hungarian by birth, and it is anticipated that a great deal will be heard of him ih London this Hummer. However, Kubelik, last year's lion, is anything but superseded or forgotten arel promises to be as popular as ever with musical connoisseurs. It is rumoured that his en- gagement list is practically full and that his services are commanding a .very high figure. The world of Theatres is now to all intents and purposes ready for the Cownation season, which should be a very remunerative one for them. The two latest productions • are &amp;like at the Adelphi and 17te President at the Prince of Wales's, neither of which have been very favourably received by the critics., Mr. Charles Hawtrey, most popular. and. polished of actors, is unfortunate in not having secured another striking piece to follow up Ads successes, but it is hoped that The President Will draw him full houses during the season, despite its unfavourable reception, as his acting has lost none of its charm.</p>
        <p>Once more our friendly rivals from the Antipodes are with us, battling for the supremacy in the world of cricket, and their presence promises to add largely to the interest of the national game this summer. This week they opened a very long, programme by opposing Dr. W. G. Grace's team, the London County Cricket Club, at the Crystal Pala id their display</p>
        <p>THE VAITv OV THE ATISTIIALTAN CHICKETEHS :</p>
        <p>LEADING MEMBERS OF THE TEAM. I, I. J. KELLY. 2, Me A. NOBLE. :3, J. DARTING. (c.4.prar:). 4, CLEM /JILL. 5. S. E GREGORY,</p>
        <p>afforded the critics a better chance of gauging their merits than the practice at the nets had done. It is..thought, upon the whole, that they ire perhaps not quite such a strong combination as they were two yeari ago, ',though it is early days yet to- judge. 'Mr. Darling is again their captain, and under his ' com- mand are serving Messrs. Hill, Kelly, Noble, Gregory, Jones and Howell, who have all pre- viously done great things against us in. 1899 and earlier campaigns: Mr. Clem Hill is being watched with particularly keen interest, as he has unques- tionably developed into one- of the finest bat% in the world, and has proved all throughhis career peculiarly successful against English bowling. He is a very finished exponent of the art of batting, scoring fast and hitting tremendauSly hard—so hard, in fact, that he laid some of his eentrilref !tore de combat at the net Every supporter of England is extremely anxious that we should render a better account of ours lves in the rubbsr of live test matcheS after' pur recent failures to cope with the' Colonials, and experts consider the English chance this season a very good one, as we can put an extremely strong representative eleven in the field. The county cricket reason opened half-a- week earlier, but began badly with drawn matches owing to the bad weather. The play so far, however, has revealed the fact that some of our cracks are in great form, notably Mr. C. B. Fry and Dr. Grace, the perennial champion.</p>
