      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>[Am Rums BaszavED.]</head>
        <p>[Am Rums BaszavED.]</p>
        <p>NUMBER 6 PARADE,</p>
        <p>"it. E. FRANCIS" (Author of "In a I%rth Country Village," 4.e.)</p>
        <p>Pane 111. Mrs. Collins had accompanied Mr. Hook, Lord Mar- tindale's lawyer, to the door and now stood on the threshold, still mechanically pressing her hands to- gether, and looking before her with unseeing eyes. She felt stunned, she could scarcely realise even yet the full extent of the sacrifice that was demanded of her. The nigger troupe had moved a little higher up the Parade, and fragments of the chorus came occasionally to her ears; the Ventriloquist was carrying on a dialogue in a squeak treble and a deep, Manly bass; the Montmorency family were still capering and ainging, the air was full of a babel of vices and laughter. Was it weeks, or months, or years since Mrs. Collins had last taken note of thee( things? A voice from the gateway recalled her to herself. "Can I step in for a minute, Mrs. Collins, to rest me? This.'ere blessed child is so heavy, it's a'most more nor I can do to carry it." It was Mrs. Whiteside and she was • carrying Martha's baby. "Our Martha and her husband are list'ning to the niggers yonder," she pursued, opening the gate and beginning to walk up the garden path, " and Joe, he's that taken with them he was all for stoppire too, but I thought it was turnin' a bit chilly for the dear child, so I told them I'd be stepping along 'ome wi' it. But it's getting that heavy, bless its little heart ! that My arms fair ache. So I thought I'd ask yon to let me sit down a minute." "To be sure," agreed Mrs. Collins with mechani- cal hospitality ; she felt as though someone else was speaking in her stead. Walking as though in a dream the led the way to the parlour and Mrs. Whiteside seating herself in the chair just vacated by the lawyer, began to unfasten the wrappings which enfolded the sleeping baby. "Isn't he a bonny boy? " she cried with irre .pressible pride as she threw back the veil from the chubby pink face. "Six weeks old the day before yesterday—and takes as much notice already as if it was six months. There's an arm for you ! and look at the length of his feet! he'll be a gradely lad— he will for sure ! " Mrs. Collins uttered some inarticulate phrase in token of admiration; it was all she could do to re- frain from bursting into tears. The other woman's Souls had pierced. through the dull nightmare-like sense of oppression which heal seemed te; enfold her. She was wide asitke now, awake and conscious of her pain. How often had she looked forward to the time when she would carry Bessie's children in her arms and fondle them as her neighbour was now fondling her grandchild; had she not even dwelt of late on the pride and joy with which she would, at no very distant date, perhaps, exhibit to admiring cronies a small flannel-wrapped bundle who would not only be flesh of her flesh and bone of her bone, but an infant Honourable, an embryo Lordling ! Oh, what would it matter, she now thought with a bursting heart, if Bessie's baby were of the common- est order—it would be her grandchild; Heaven knew if she would ever be permitted to nurse Bessie's children. She could find it in her heart to envy Mrs. Whiteside. "I s'pose you're awful busy getting Bessie's things ready;" remarked the visitor presently, rock- ing the baby on her parted knees. "Your sister-in- law, Jane, tolti. me you'd got her a lovely silk dress for the weddin'. I was say ta' to her as it was a wonder you didn't have white satin. The lord would have shared the expense, wouldn't he ?" " He would if he was let," returned iMrs. Collins with asperity. Let her trouble be what t might she would hold up her head before folks. "I'm not one of the kind as 'ud let anybody but myself pay for my daughter's clothes before she's wed. I had a gray silk dress for my own weddin' and Bessie she always said she'd like to be married in a gray dress too. It's a beautiful bit of silk—l doubt if • she'll ever wear better." ' "Eh, I'd like to see it. I'd ask ye to let me step upstairs with ye only I'm frightened of disturb- in' the blessed child." ' "Well, I could fetch it down to you,",saia Mrs. Coffins, and she left the room with lagging uncer- tain steps. The dress was duly exhibited and ad- mired. Mrs. Whiteside indeed being so enthusiastic that him Collins experienced an odd, faint glow of elation and pleasure. "I was half thinkin' of letting her wear my. brooch; my big brooch you know, Mrs. Whiteside, wi' the purple red stones. But Bessie won't hear on it. She says she won't take it off me while I'm alive. But I'll leave it to her in my will," she added, thinking with a mournful satisfaction that it could never be said that Lady Martindale had no jewellery coming to her from her own side of the house. "Well, I must be goin'," said Mrs. Whiteside presently. "Me and babywill be like to have the place to ourselves a bit longer I think, but I don't mind that if I can keep him quiet till his mother comes. Lord! I wouldn't ask no better company than baby! lam fair silly about him—l tell Martha sometimes she'll have to drive me out of the house—l'm as much there as at home. Mayhap by this time next year you'll be a grandmother too, Mrs. Collins. Good afternoon and thank you. Come, inseprpcions, we's be steppin'." Mrs. Collins went with her into the hall and watched her descend the stbps and walk down the path ; her eyes following the little white bundle as long as it was in sight. Then she re-entered the _ parlour and sank into the nearest chair. Through the open window came the sound of Miss Anita Mon- rnorency's voice, straining itself to the utmost. The words were oddly appropriate, "I'm goin' to be xnerried to a Mark-is or a Earl. All the lads are mad about old Joe's girl." Mrs. Collins put her fingers to her ears and went on hinking, or try- nes to think, gazing fixedly meanwhile at the silken folds of Bessie's wedding dress. Could she make up her mind to do this thing? To go away from the old place which she had never left for more than a day in he life. To go away amongst strangers— people who knew nothing of her, nor of her family. It would not seem like Sunday if she did not go to Chuch in the dear old square building yonder where she had been christened and married, and where -week after week, and year after year, she had Mt in the same place, seeing the same homely familiar faces, meeting the same old friends on the threshold— friends who had grown up and grown old with Mrs. Collins herself. There had &amp;deed been many little bickerings and heart burnings between them in the past, but at this moment of impending separation she felt she loved them all, even Mrs. Whiteside, though she did make herself so disagreeable the week before last in the market. The market ! how would it be possible to do one's marketing in a new place where people did not know Mrs. Collins and might runup their prices Perhaps they would not even give her discount, as everyone did here, because she was such an old customer. Her eyes wandered round theoom—thohttle shabby familiar rOom. She did not realise its shabbiness, she only remembered how she and Col- lins used to sit there, one on each side of the hearth, after the day's work was done ; and Bessie, a little toddling thing, used to play between them, and Collins used to tell 'her how he had got on at busi- ness, and she used to give an account of her day. How proud they had been when they furnished the drawing-ropm floor and were able to take their first set of lodgers, it would be very queer having no lodgers to do for—she would feel lost without her „work, but she would not mind that if only she might stay on at Number 6. For twenty-five years and more she had been Mrs. Collins of Number 6—it seemed to her that she would not be herself when she ceased to be Mrs. Collins of Number 6. At first, the thought of this uprootal seemed in a manner to deaden the yet more poignant thought of the sep- aration from Bessie—a separation.wh, her instinct told her would be more complete than The lawyer had hinted: Her heart rose up in fierce rebellion,—the cruelty of it—the meanness ! She, herself, was to become the instrument by which the plan was to be serried out—the plan which was to rob her of eVery, thing she valued in life. " They reckon I'll do it because I love my gun," Mrs. Collins said to herself. " Well, I do love her— but there's things a body shouldn't be asked to do. There's things as isn't in nature." The clock ticked on while she sat pondering and strugglinzwith herself. The time was getting shOrt now. Mr. Hook would be back din cely, and her mind was no nearer being made up than when he 4pft. She could not make up her ; mind now—they must wait—she would write But meanwhile thei • wry! Bessie's dress lying on the sofa. Mr. Hook must not find it'there—it would not be seemly. She rose, mechanically smoothitM out the folds. It suited Bessie rarelye As she slowly mounted the stairs she thought of B'essie's bright face when she had tried •it on completed, of how she had come dancing across the floor, holding up the rustling skirt and crying: "Am I not a lucky girl, mother? I am the happiest girl in the world I think." Mrs. Collins pauSed on the landing :—" She'd break her heart, I believe, if was disappointed now." Mullet Mr. Heok in a tee' minutes later, Drecedin'</p>
        <p>Juni into tie parlbur- vend mei-dons, r-jtat so u to face him'.</p>
        <p>turning</p>
        <p>do it, sir," she said, "it's 'ad--Ws anal 'aid, but I'll do it."</p>
        <p>Mr. Hook was taken aback as much by the sud- flemiess of the announcement as-by the fierceness oi her tone and the smouldering light of her eyes. He was not an imaginative man, and it did not occur tc him that this poor woman was a heroine, or that her sacrifice had in it anything sublime. He thought it was, as she said, hard, but he was relieved that she made so little fuss.</p>
        <p>" Well, Mrs. Collins," he said, with a great as- sumption of cheerfulness. "I an glad to hear you say this—very glad. lam quite sure you will not regret it. You have by this—ah—simple act of— all—hum—renunciation secured your daughter's- happiness for life." "I hope so I'm sure," returned Mrs. Collins, her cheeks suddenly glowing, and that fire in her eyes leaping out. "I hope so I'm sure; but I can't say as I can think any man—let him be who he may— aught but a poor creature --who can behave so. To take my daughter off me as he's doin'—you know he means to do it, sir—is bad enough. I know lam but a working woman but I love my gun l as dear as if I was the Queen. And she is my gun, and the Lord made me her mother, soit's downright Ilyin' in the face of Providence to make me give ter up. And then to drive me away from my home, and make me promise to deceive her and to pretrd I'm doin' it of my own free will, and not because his Lordship's ashamed of me.—What's that? Eh, dear! Bessie, how you frightened me! I never heard you come in." There indeed stood Bessie, with her dark eyes dilated, and all the pretty colour bleached from her face.</p>
        <p>"Mother," she said tremulously, "what was it you were saying abeut Algernon making you give ,me up, and driving you from your home ?' Here was a pretty kettle of fish! While the lawyer was casting about in his mind for any words which might explain away the situation Mrs. Col- lins rushed to the rescue. Ilkd not Mr. Hook been so much annoyed and perturbed he must 'have been touched at the manner by which the poor creature sought to defend the man who had plotted against her happiness, and to make it appear as though the arrangement he proposed were the most natural thing in'the world. She had but spoken in jest,— the mendaciously assured Bessie ; well, perhaps not altogether in jest—as the girl indignantly refuted this statement—she might have been a bit vexed for the moment, but now that she came to think of it she quite agreed with his Lordship. It was most natural that he should object to her living so near, and perhaps it would really be for the best if she moved, somewhere where people didn't laiow her and "douldn't case up at her as she had made her living letting lodgings. Mr. Hook, recovering him- self, joined in and between them they made out a beautiful case for Lord Martindale. Bessie listened in silence, but with so strange an expression, that by and by her mother went to her, laying her hand upon her arm : " You've been a bit ,upset, my dear," she said, "but don't you take it to heart. It's all for the bed."</p>
        <p>'Yes, it's all for the best," repeated Bessie, rous- ing herself. "I am not upset, mother. lam sur- prised—that's all. Are you returning to Lord Mar- tindale this evening, sir ?" she enquired addressing the lawyer. Mr. Hook signified that he was.</p>
        <p>"Then will you be so kind as to give him a mess- age from me?" She went away hastily, presently returning with a parcel which she was fastening up as she crossed the room. "Oh, I have forgotten one thing," she said, depositiuig the packet on the table and untying the string. Within lay'a, variety of jewel cases. Bessie drew off her ring, the beau- tiful diamond engagement ring, of which she and her mother had been so proud, and slipped it into one of them.</p>
        <p>"Will you kindly. return these to Lord Martin- dale ?" she said turning to Mr. Hook. "And tell him I never wish to see him again." "Bessie, Bessie, think what you are saying," cried Mrs. Collins aghast. " Eh, dear, I'd sooner have bitten my tongue off nor say a word which should make you do this. Nay, my dear, .I'm an old woman and happen not long fbr this world, and I'd like to leave you 'appy ! You'll break your heart, Bessie—l'd rather do anything or go any- where than let you break your heart for me., ' "I won't break my heart, mother," replied Bessie. "Lord Martindale is not the man thought him—l couldn't love him any more. I could never be happy with a man who treated my mother so." "Come, come, come, my dear young lady," cried Mr. Hook in great perturbaticm4 4. pow ,aeteamer serious. You know his Lordship is devoted to you—" "It's a funny sort of devotion then," said Bessie, "to begin with deceiving me and being so cruel to the person I love best in the 'world. Here is the parcel, sir. Perhaps you will kindly take it and go away."</p>
        <p>Aiter many protests Mr. Hook withdrew' carrying the parcel with Mm; and then Bessie threw her arms round the neck of her weeping mother. "Don't cry, mother," she said, clasping her close. "You loved me first and you loved me best —you have sacrificed yourself for me. lam glad I found it out in time. I'm not crying, see," said Bessie very bravely. "You and I will be very happy together, mother, and nothing or nobody shall .ever part us." . _</p>
        <p>But though Bessie was so valiant, I am not sure that she did not shed many tears in secret over her lost dreams and her lost lover.</p>
        <p>Lord Martindale blusiered and protested, and en- treated; but aIL in vain. Bessie would have none of him. It was a little hard at first to endure the astonished and repeated quaries of relations and neighbours; but the nine days' wonder came to an end and by and by Mrs. Collins began to feel almost as much pride in relating how Bessie had refused his Lordship as she originally had in announcing her engagement to him. Ultimately Bessie married Sam, and Mrs. Collins insisted on the young couple taking up their abode with her.. She may frequently be seen on fine days parading up and down in the sun- shine with a flannel-wrapped bundle in her arms, and there is not a more prosperous or united family in Witham than that which occupies Number Parade.</p>
