      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>PLAGUE OF DEAFNESS.</head>
        <p>A PLAGUE OF DEAFNESS.</p>
        <p>The statistics of Aural Specialists and of Stricial Clinics devoted to the trestmetr of ear diseases show that ther la an ahrming increase in the number of people wh) are alined by-deafness. The cause of this increasing Prsvilenne of deective hearing is attributed by the mi-d cal professieln to a greater tendency of late years to cattarrhal ailments. At the Drouet Institute careful observations of the history of the cases under treatment veritr.s this opinion, as more thin half the p,tienis alribute the cause of 'heir deafness to takieg cold, or to catarrh of the nose and throat extending to the ears. •</p>
        <p>An instance of an obstinate form of defective beer- Mg, attributed to such, a cause, is contained in the fol- lowing extract' from the recores of the Institute : Mr P E.de, who sLffered from deafness for 12 years, states that the ear troutls originated in a violent cold which settled hi the head and ears This patient ales had noises in t`le 'head, discharges from the ears, and a catarrh of the noao aid throat. Io fact, complioations of the most severe c•iaraster were present in this case, and yet the cure effectea by the Drt.uet method was complete. _ . _ _ _ .</p>
        <p>Clacton-road, SP. Opyth. Nr C,lchester, Marcb 14th, 1902. . _</p>
        <p>Dear Sir,—l an, glad to say that my hearing is guts good tow. I don't want to hear any better. I can hear as well now as I could 13 years ago, therefore I don't think I shall need any furtier trettinimt or advice. Ever, thing is quite clear now; my throat and the nostrils are quite cured. Thanking you for your kind- nee3 and what I call a complete and radical cure.— Yours respectfully,</p>
        <p>PRESION E4DR.</p>
        <p>Those who are interested in Deafness, or diseae• s of the ear, no', nr throat, can obtain a free cony of Lb,* "Journal for the Deaf," with a" Supplement" contain- ing detailf d act:sonata of 40 oases cured daring the past moan, by eddrersing the S*crettra. ' Dermot Ineptute, Regent's Park•road, London, N.W. Each joutoal is accompa,ied by a Patient's Report Firm, which ellthies applicants to receive full particulars for applying the treatment at home. Any advice by correapoitdence is absolutely free.</p>
        <p>Wild Flowers at the Warrington Museum. , For several years now av attempt bail been made to interest the younger pr,rtion of the visitors to the Museum in the subject of botany. One means adopted has been the exhibition of fresh specimens ot common wild flowers; The flowers, which are renewed weekly, are placed at the west end of the Art Gallery, and are carefully labelled, With occa. sional explanato,fy notes and diagrams. The exhibi. tion for the present season commenced a few week, ago, and the following species are now on view Lesser celandine (ranunculus &amp;aria), marsh mar" gold (caltha paluetris), May-flower (cardamine pratensis), red campion (lyehnis diurna), Win- nower (stellaria holostea), shepherd's purse (cap- sella burscpastoris), gorse (ulex europaeus),groutid ivy (nepeta glechoma), dog's mercury (marcurialis perennis), ash (fratimis excelsior), black poplar (populous nigra), gust willow (seal caprea). This list does not. profess to be exhaustive even for the mottlent,but with a little outside assistanP,e, which would be very welcome, the display could be made [ sufficiently repre.entative.</p>
        <p>The Military Ball at Parr Hall. The net profits of the military ball, held in Parr Hall, on the Bth April,l in aid of the Floldiers' and Sailors' ramilie%' Association,amoul2t to £123 38 4d, the proceeds of the ball reslising £56 ls 44 and the donations £67 28. The result must, be very gratify- ing to the bon secretary, Mrs George Dakin.</p>
