        <p>- At the Warrington eonce Court on httelltia• Joseph Howard aged nineteen, was charged with ob,eining from Joseph Throssell, 13. Legh-street, food and lodgings to the amount of 15s by false pretences. He pleaded guilty. The C, let Constable stated that between nine ,nd ten &amp;Wank on April 23 the prisoner Caine to r Throssell $ house in search of lodgings. He was a perfect szanaer. but eventually terms were agreed upon. Prisoner said he was a clerk in the geode department of the Londoe and N int] Western deli- way and had bean transferred fium Rugby to Wertington. He added that he was a mambas of ,he Young Man's Christian A.seeclation, and that a member of that body had pointed out idr Th,essell's house to him. When asked about his 'uggage he said that it had helm delaye ton the way Ile stased at the houoc until Saturday, and used t't go out as though going to his bliejUmBs, and return. 41a Saturday Mr hrosseu had his suspicions aroused, and on the prisoner going out, saying a hat he was going for his wages, prose utor tot owed hint ana found he simply wet for a stroll ato g Wilson Pattena.treet In- formed° a was given to the pollee, and it was found that he was not employed on !the London and sorth-Westasra Rail- way, our was he a member ,af the Yo tag Reis Chri.-tian Association. The oniy account hew give of himself was that he was nineteen years of age,and was a Merit frown Man- Chestet. The henCh toflicted a Ana of .tOs i,1,,u tang mute, Or fourteen days imprisonment in he almond division,</p>
