        <p>_ . GOING TO TEN Recce —Thomas Keegan Blackburn, was charged with travelling on the London and' ortb-Wettern Railway from Blackburn to Warrington,without having paid his fare. on Tueaday morning —Kr tspinali. station-ma ter at Bank Quay Static, , istated that he Wee watching t,.6 eolleoting of the tickets from the p 'engem on the excursion train to Chester races from roine,which stopped at Warring- ton at • quarter to eleven fl • sew the prisoner (VI out and run along the platform Witness was barring the way to prevent the pas•eogers on that half of the train getting mixed with . hose wh ~la tickets had been collected. Fle stopped the prisoner ant asked him for his ticket. He said be had given it up to a collector. and witness then asked him to produee his return half. He said his" pal" had it in the carrtae. Witleffee went to the carriage indicated in whio 1 there were seven or eight per- sons but none would own t spewing prisoner, who after- wards admitted that he id had no ,ioket Detective Howell stated that his attei% w ton was drawn to the ewe. R 5 questioned the prisoner. who owned that he had neither money nor ticket He said he ecb got on the train at Black- burn and that a friend had peemised to gel two tiekets The train started before his friend came, and as the train did not stop before reach! ,g Warricutan he ortuld not get off Use ked witness to wait until the nEt train came in to see if his f,ie id wee on it P,lsoner adhered to this statement, but duDerintendent Misnagh.in expressed ,he opinion that the man was having " a cheap ride to the raoes."—Pri,oner was fined 20e, or in default 14 days Impriscr,ment _ . . ...... . .. ... . . .</p>
