        <p>The Late Councillor Plumpton.</p>
        <p>'I On Saturciey evening Councillor Mullen distributed • the re zes geine I in the Coronetion billiard haneicap at the W rd, woe Liberal Club, Bewsey-street. Before ,I the distribu in to ,k pl-ce, Mr W JEFEREYs In ,ved a vote of condolence with the tele, iyes ,1 the late Councillor Plumpton, who wes a vice-president of the club. He amid be was sure they would all feel the solemn. Ica ef the occesion. The late Councillor Plurnpton. had be, nii aid out amongst them for a g:eat number of ye. re. He was a man who wee not very ofeeetetious silo oil me pu-le himself to the front, but was one ef -oeblient workers wee was pl •aged to da geed by hedth an f ii (he speaker) waa very much inclined to think that he had in the end found fame by his work. When tee,' locked farmed and E4Vf the numbers of their ted Mends who weie being tektia away from theen, it made them feel a little bit sad and ask the Tiestien " Who is going to step into the ranks 7" He ehould be very pleased if they could al- ways, as lied Id en the ca.) in the war in South Africa, , have metnbtra reedy to step up and fill any vacancies in the reeks. When they looked around and remenabered the number of their oil ye' orana who had hen in the forefront of .lie battle in Warrington for the last fifty years, aril ..aw One •fter •norher falling, they did not es,. the venue men taking the same intere,t in the work, diselas in the same amount of energy, and trying to do the seme amount of good, that their old friends lied done.' H., hoped now that many of these young men would come out and buckle on the arineur, and ,se if they could not alter the state of things in Warrington. The late Councillor Piumpton did as well as he could. He was a man who was always plodding, N./nil successful as a businese man, and he (the speakre) thought partly successful as a politician. Therefore they ought at least to pass a deep Yteti of • sympathy wi, h the sons he had left behind, and be (the speaker) hoped they would worthily walk in the foot- steps of their father and do their portien for the benefit of Warrington. Mr WARREN, as a member of the cdminittee,seconded. He rem*, ked that he had been inturietely in touch with tiie latr C einceler Piumpton for corn., time and could bear net ell that Mr Jeffroys had said in reference to his cod ch•recter. Councillor Pturripton had been con- nected with • be club for many years as vice-preeodent— he heliewd from 1886—sod during the whole of that time he had de,eted reat tune and energy to the farther- ance of Liber .1 principles and the imprevernent of that club. AT' rh• hi conifer, when the club was in low wa'er tin stuck to it through' thick and thin, and when the club reaohdd its weemt sound position no one rejoiced in its success mere than Councillor Plum. ten. He was a man • who was always yriefeg to do the best he could, and anything ich bad for its object tbe welfare of the members of the club,receiee4 tie cqrdial suppart.fie was a thorough, hroad-mieded Liberal,tieencumte•red with any feels.•nd we always w irking for nis party. go wee genial in di/018010n, level headed in business, and by hie claath not only the Liberal party, but the town of Wsrrington, had °Uff,ro,l • e e it ins +. . .</p>
        <p>. . _ TINNION 4180 Pp,ke aud said that the late C uneillor p.utnot ,n toik an irViereat in every assecia- tioo which +.04.1 far the beaefit of Ins fellow towsiam.n. We (the speilte• ) was very closely aseociated svih him on rieeeral committees of the Town C•turmil, Red he had 'oh sit.aii-in in saving that the V7arrington Town C mead had lost one of its most uaeful members. Couecillor Plump*. -in wee a man who had the courage of hia ocinioos .04 was ds'airmined i hat right should ha done. 11 wis afraid ad.rio ofili an leog as lie knew he avii in the rieht and at many timel and on man/ CUM- rni,teaq had h • brought f award and probed maners that he nons;de,e t were not as demirable as they might hove he' M nv tim, a hia energy had been wasted be- cawie thini.ts were not ao ad as be thought, but wh,,never h ft- there w.s a wrong or injustice, or any- •hinz thtt w•e no for tho good of the town being don., W4S n‘vet afraid .0 lif' his voice. As far ac the mem- berg nf rh it alai) wigs coocern,d, Councillor Plurnpton oupp,rted them not only by hie membership, but by his ! pria.enc:s, and tin psi• fII amide:masa of his death 1 raVe hit le s more oev,r,lir felt than if he ha i bad a lingering Hoops. He v 7.4 amen; them six daye bawd' h • died a d hi. death was a Painful shock t, them. The on'e o ag•epd te in sil.nce. ' Councillnr TINNION then. s..id he had pleasure in dis- tributing the helia d orig.' on behalf of Mr Arthur Gres- field, t stir prasident, wki wag unavo'd•bly &amp;bane throogh enua,,ement with the g -If club at Floylake. The f I iwina is a list of the prze winners :-1, gold &amp;linnet, G 9 Dar'on ; 2, electro tea pat end biscuit hartel, J ; 3 r•adirg lame, Dr R Smith ; 4, oily er cisatet'e coc, W Whittaker ; 5, cue and case (preoneted he C Pieruniet), J Gra-me ; 6, cue and ease (rire”dr.d by Mr W Bayliff), E V Walker ; 7, ale r mounted carvers, J Bowen ; 8, arue', A Green- halgh ; 9, toast tack, 13 Leck,tt. ; 10, carvers, E Wilson: 11, carvers, A.