        <p>WARRINGTON. The Catholic Association.</p>
        <p>The prizes won in the recent billiard handicap in connection with this association wore distributed on Monday evening at the rooms of the R9BoeißtiOtl as follows :-1, A Connor, cue and case ; 2, B Mac- donald,briar and amber pipe with case; 3, M Flynn watch; 4 G Baton, pipe and case. During the evening opportunity was taken of presenting the Rey Father Crowe. who recently left Warrington to take up the curacy of St Peter's Seal-it-teat, Liver- pool, with a solid silver chalice. The Rev Father Crowe afterwards presented to Mr Owen Toole a gold-mounted umbrella, on behalf of the members of the association, in recognition of his services to the ascoliation, r.nil as captain of the billiard team ' The remainder of the evening WaS spent in a most enjoyable manner, the following contributing songs, k,c ; Professor McGregor, Niessrs Toole, J Flynn, J B Newnit, C .T Newton,P Tle,rry,T Gilligan, IL A Sanders, M Cunningham and Gleeson, end the Warringtm Borough Band, whese selections were especially meritorous. Oa the motion of Mr Boyle, seconded by Mr Berry, , the artistes were cordially thanked for their services.</p>
        <p>Funetal of the Late Mr. George Beard, Sen. The remains of the late Mr George Beard, sea, who died at his residence, in Priestley-street, after a protracted illness, on tte 2nd inst, were interred in the cemetery on Monday afternoon, the officiat- ing minister being the Rev C II Hocken. The prin- cipal mourners were Messrs H Beard and George Beard (sons), 111e,•srs Charles Nurse. F Hied, and E J Parker (nephews), and Messrs Joseph Fletcher, Jozeph Bunch, J Lawton, George Morrell, James Ashton, F Taylor, J Fieldhouse, John Houghton and W Ward. Several other friends were present at the graveside. The coffin, which was a handsome one with bras mountina.,, was supplied by Mr Lowden 'Rene. The deceased was much respected by a large circle of friends. Several very beautiful wreaths had been forwarded by relatives and friends. Mr. Luke Fildes' State Portrait. _ „ _ _</p>
        <p>In the " World " foi7th—e---c-nri:er-it..;,;ek Lady Colin Campbell roviews some of the Royal Academy pictures. Her reference to Mr Luke Fades' State portrait is as follows In the big room the place of hmour is of curse occupied by Mr Luke Fades State portrait, "His Majesty, King Xdward the ' Seventh." State portraits, "painted by commana," have usually a paralysing effect on an artist's powers; and this one, which is both insigoificent and coarse, is no exception to this regrettable rule. It is more of a relief than usual to pass en from this pompous and futile work to the beautiful reticence of Mr Saraeot's portrait of " Mrs Endi- cott," a charming dignified oil Puritan American lady, dressed in black velvet and seated in a chair.</p>
