        <p>Dr Peacocks repeated the evidence which he gave at the inquest, read added Wet neelect would aggravate the diee. hi answer te Aldermen Francomb, witness said he had no hesitation in saying that the child had been neglected. Thomas Goulding, son of the female priaoner, again gave evidence. Be said he weet to the prisoner's house an Monday night and found the chill dengerously The man was " speechless drunk ' and witnese gave him a good thrashing. The fe.nale prisoner had given way to drinking,but the man Was the worse of the two. Witness thrashed him because of the feaeful couditien of the child. There was is thing in the house for the child excent a drop of milk and a small quantity of Witness told the men to send for mere brandy ' but he would mot. Mrs Mackie, a neiehlemr, said ehe nashed the chili on Monday. She was glad to get it off her knee because she saw it was dying. With regard to the negligeace witness stated that prisoners were equally to blame. The male prisoner made a staterneut to the Bench and a (=tiled the woman of heing.of drunken habits. . . _ .</p>
