      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>WIDNES CHILD MURDER;</head>
        <p>WIDNES CHILD MURDER;</p>
        <p>Sentence at the Assizes.</p>
        <p>At Liverpool Anima, On S .turdsr, Le`are Mr Justice Walton, the Widnes child murder ease came on. The prisoner iA Catherine Whelan, .aged 33, and she was Charged with the wilful murder of her infant eon James, aged eight months, at 5, Widnes, on March 20. Prisoner and her husband, it was stated, had quarrelled, when he crack her •ad left her. Prisoner then in desty;ir drowned tier child in a dolly tub contUhin tem inch, of water in the backyard. She put an earthenware mu,-; on the top of the tub. Sins atterward3 gave herself up to the police. The woman was found insane, and was ordered to be detained in a criminal lunatic asylum.</p>
        <p>The New Postal Motors.—Accident at Knotty Ash. Od Friday night, a breakdown of one of the especial motor•cars which have been purchased by the postal authorities to take midnight mails from Liverpool to Manchester occurred at Knotty Ash about 10-45. The machinery from some reason got out of order, and the horse van, which was in attendance, took all the baggage which it was possible to &amp;Out extra vans had also to be engaged to take the whole of the load. The baggage was then taken on to Warrington and afteerwarda to Manchester by the old method of hone propulsion. Needless to say, the accident caused great incon- venience and delay.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>DEATH OF A WARRINGTON CHILD. Inquest anci Verdict.</head>
        <p>THE DEATH OF A WARRINGTON CHILD. Inquest anci Verdict.</p>
        <p>The inqu.st touching tu death of El zsbeth Alice Gouldine, the two yetr old ch 11e.f Harry Walters and Selina Goulding, Bax'er'e-yard, was conducted at tho Waringtcn Police Stirion on Siturday rooming by Mr S Brighouse, County Coroner. Prior to the inquest tie patents were again brought before the magiatratis on , tie °barge of n.glect and were toronaliy rernkrined ul Monday. Prisoners were preset at the inquest.—Mr Browne watched toe proceedings on behalf of the N.S. P.O C.</p>
        <p>- At toe outset the Cnemer related the facts of the case to the jury. EN said the child, which was net quite two years of age died at cher Union Heepital ou the pre- ViStle Wednesday. It app&amp;ared that the perente were both of drunken habits and in April of last year Inspector Ilackezt had them under his ohtervation ae Lie thoupt they were neglecting the child. In August WI lost trace of them. Oa Tuesday morning, April 29'4, from information he received he went to a house in Baxter's Yard and saw the child which was very ill. and appeared to have been neglected. Dr Peacocke was called in and ordered the child's removal to the Workhouse Eiespital wherp it died the next day. The parents were token into custody, and the jury would have to sty whelher there tied been neglec% of such a criminal, culpable, and geese nature as to justify them in tending toe prisoners for trial for manslaughter. Thomas G trifling, a cotter, residing at 27, VViandok- road, eon of the female prisoner, said his in:grier was a widow, but lived with Waltere in 'Baxter':-.rard. Wit- ness saw the child alive on Monday night when he went to the house, because be had been told that We mother was very poorly. About three weeks or a month aga witness taok the child and kept it for several days, because be saw it was being neglected. Witness further stated that Walters had ill-used Mrs Goulding.</p>
        <p>Dr Peacocke said he was called to see the Child on the previous Tuesday about noon, cod found it lyiog on a box near the fire. Tue child seemed to be very ill and was in r very emaciated condition. It was covered with flea bites, and was too ill fcr witneert of make an exliaostive ex:amination. Ids ordered the child's removal to the hospital. Both prisoners were in the house when he visited it, and were sober. The man was eating name bread and meat, and the only other toed in the house was a drop of milk in a cup. Toe child died at the hospital, and witneal and Dr Braid e made a post mortem examination. They did not flad any evidence in tie pest mortem to had them to nay that the chi d had been neglected. The cause of death was tubermilar (meningitis and tulonrole of the lungs, the disease, in witnese's opinion, being hereditary. By Mr Browne : The child weighed only 12Ibn Ezs, whereas it should have weighed 24 or 251 be. The diaease, if hereditary, would probably account tor that. Dr Braids gave evidence of a similar character. Rely- ing upon the post-mortem examination and wsat ti saw of the child before its death, he wee not role to say that death was trmght about by negleut. Tho child was in such a dissased eoadition that it might have died if the greatest care hail teen bsatowed upon it. Nurse Coach gave evideace ci to the child's death. The Coroner said that in his opi,lion the jars need net pursue the inquiry any further, lie sugg.steu that they should confine their vardicL to the bare statement of the doctors, and in his opinion t iere will not suffici.et ey,- denies to justify them in sending the prisoners for trial. no the other had. there might be sufent evilence to justify the magistrates dealing with them on the charge of negleet,but the jury had not to deal with that matter. A verdict in accordance with the medical testimony was returned.</p>
        <p>Addressing tbe prisoners, the Coo* r said that if the jury and himself were setisfled that prisoner would not or brought before the magistrades, they would have hid something to gay which perhaps they (prisoners) weuli net like. Ho wanted to impress upeo them that the miry reason why they refrained from doing so was because they did net wish to prjudien prisoners when they came before the magistrate,. FURTHER M kGISTERIAL PROCEEDINGS. SMART SENTENCE ON THE PRISONERS.</p>
        <p>The prison( ri, Harry Walters and S 3 Has Gintfding, were again brought beLre the magivrates on tdond•y oa the charge of neglrctieg their child, in such a manger as to cause it unnecessary suffering and isjury to health. I —Mr Browns prosecuted, sod brit.fly outlined the cm. Cooper gave evidence of his visit te the house in I company with e.o. Taylor en Tuesday night in conse- quence of the woman's allegation that the man had been asseultinz her. The man was lying on the fieor in a carnet of the kitchen drunk. and the child, wnich appeared to be very ill, was lying on an old couch. The wociaan was al3o under tho influence of drill. Ti matter was reported to Inspector Hackett. Inspector thckett stated that on April 24th of last year the man brought the chid to him at his office. On examining the child witnces found it to-be in a horribly filthy state and swarming with lice. The wornan promised to clean the child and make amends. Witness supervised the case until Jane 11th and the woman kept her promise. He then lost sight of prisonirs, but in August found them lodging at another woman's (souse. The child was then very healthy and clean and was being cared for. He did net see it again until the previous Wednesday evening:when he visited the bolas in Baxter's-yard in consequence of a report from the police</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>I THE CORONATION. i</head>
        <p>I THE CORONATION. i</p>
        <p>More Arrangements for Local Festivities.</p>
        <p>D u rroN.</p>
        <p>A committee meeting in connection with the forth- coming Coronation festivities was held in the Board School the other eveeinz, when th.re werg present— , Messrs Robert Fowles (chalrmao), J, s,ph Baxter, Her- . beet Potts. Peter Mounficld, Samuel Kinsey JAWS Cooke. Joseph Smith, Harold Frith, and the Secretary (Mr Sydney E Stokes). On the proposition of Mr Baxter, seconded by Mr Smith, Friday. the 27,11 June, was fired, instead of Thursday, 26-h, as previously arranced, tor the celebration. Miss Smi k, New Brook Farm, Dutton, having agree t ) cater tor the tea, the comma tee discussed several details in connection with sports, fez., when the Secretary reported that it was the unanimous wish of the township of Bartington to join !Dutton in the festivies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>THELWALL.</head>
        <p>THELWALL.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the parishioners was held in the National Schools. on Monday evening, to consider what steps should be taken to celebrate the Coronation of the Kiug,—Mr N Taylor, ctuurcuan of the Parish Council, presided.—The Rev T Pym Wi.liamson delivered a short speech, and pro- pots.] that the Tnelwall ce eheatiou should consist of two parts r—The first to be a " Cbildren's thy." (Hear, hear.) Children were the custodiaus of history, and perhaps, 70 years hence they would be able to say that they could remember the Coronation of King Elwarcl VII. As iegards the second part, he had been anxious to find neutral ground to which all could take an interest. He thought they-would ficci It in the church- yard, for that was a place in which every soul in the parieh had an inter•,e. He prop is d that a " Lych Gate ' should be erected. Some stetter was greatly needed for the mourners who had to bury their dead in inclement weather, and be thought it would be u,eful for the whole parish. It was a curious c-Ancidence that Edward the Elder, who loon led the ancient " city " of Thee wall (and lived therg fwr Crime time) became King in 901, and was crowned in 902 ; and King Edward VII became King in 1901, arid would be crowned in 1902. He so ges!ed that a plate c menetneratin2 these two events sheuld be placed,on the" Lych Gate."—Aclmiral. Jones-Pa:l7 quite agreed wit o the Vicar's suggestions. He thought there would be no diffiaulty about the matter and he would give the land upoa which he suggested the Lych Gate should be srected.—Ail present declared themselves in favour of the schemes.—A committee was elected to carry out thaairingemeats.</p>
        <p>In the grounds of Hitchin Workhouse on Wednesday a horse attached to the carriage of a member of Board of Guardians bolted, and knockd down three old women, one being killed on the spot and the others aliverelv injured. The Calais correspondent of the " DaCy News," says : —A novelty in weddings is reported from Bethune, where on the same dare in the family g, lieu, silver, and premier weddings were colebra ed, the whole party attending the parish church. The grandfs. her and grandmother of the bridegroom, aged 83 and 78 respec- tively, celebrated the fiftieth annivt-rsary of their wed- ding, vii-,1!0 the bridegr O's rno'.her and father cele- brated their 25th Year of wedded lug.</p>
        <p>An cap.ouon occurred on trard a German torpedo boat at Walnolmsbaveu on Wednesday, and two men were killed. According to a Parliamentary return Cneshire has six boroughs with a population of io,ooe, Cumberland three. Derbyshire three, Laacashire nineteen, Sarcpshire two, and Westmoreiand one.</p>
