      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>EXAMINER.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>MY 9th 1902.</p>
        <p>ENGLISH people who Queen Wilhelmina. have so lately mourned</p>
        <p>the death of good Vxmome will have read with a special nil of pleasure the good news which has come le'ra Holland. Despite the caution of the phy- 3Leians in their bulletins, the officials at the Dutch l'egation in London felt empowered by their 14iv3te message to say that Queen WILHELMINA 73 43 considered to be out ;of danger. It would 3°o caused terrible sorrow to her subjects if the /13uthful sovereign's cateer had been cut short; 14cl although her condition must continue to etl4llle anxiety for some little time to come, it is 8.43? to understand the relief which the announce- will cause. The QUEEN has a special inter i',4t for the British race, because she is the last 014'341 descendant of that illustrious House of rtrallge which did so much for our country at a :11eat005 time in its history. There are other 14t's which explain the friendly interest of chirigland in Holland and its QUEEN. For tee :-'atch, Englishmen have always cherished a feel- of admiretion. Their prowess on sea and laud e qualities that appeal to the British people. 44ikewise their domesticity, frugality, and even litftutioss win respect among us which even the I,l4i3gUided stubbornness of their Boer kinsfolk does 0 obliterate Queen WILUELMINA herself also 43 a personal charm for us. A girlish monarch— :sPeetacle of so much sweetness in a position the much responsibility—will always appeal to efti chivalry of mankind. Queen WILEIELMINA ii-r`V gave indication of a strength of character 11\Lilltual in a woman so young. The South African r tested the capacity and the discretion of the ihee. ---N and her advisers in the early days of her wag°. She has endured the test well. Every effort made to embroil her Government on tini 130-3 of the blood relationship of her people to the Afe;r3. If she had been indifferent to the South War she would have been accused of cal- but she would have been still more 14;341e if she had allowed emotion to get the ati ;bt of her reason. Her action in providing th51):Illm for Mr KRUGER and the remnant of 'euth African Government sufficiently dis- ttehd her sympathy; but on the other hand she e%:tlied her Ministry in adherence to neutrality hi)teqards the actual combat. It is no secret that htiii°ro the outbreak of the war the young Queen kltt her advisors did their best to induce Mr a (14R to make reasonable concessions, and 3t, after the long agony of the war, there is no thet Dr KUYPER'S unofficial visit te Eng- ,: was made with her Majesty's hearty approval tat her desire. The Duch people may well tht teod of their glean, and we may rejoce with 111 that the crisis appears to have passect, and With them that her Majesty may soon re- health and strength.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>MR. BRYCE, one of Education Bin. the highest authorities</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>ti or</head>
        <p>ti or</p>
        <p>on education in this 1114 -, Moved the rejection of the Education I,4th an the motion for sec3nd . reading %use of Commons on Monday afternoon. ilile,ttinent of the Bill was most searching and and his declaration . that it would be Vttil"" ia its entirety by the Liberal party drew thiisiastic cheers from the Opposition INta;:. R e complained, and with justice, that 63tahll,t advantages wore derivable from the ftltkei'hlad'ktiltqlt of a single authority were out- ': :1:1:1 the serious disadvantages inherent in p 4tle,h""e'd ado ted by the Government, where by 111)etiel-'t work for two bodies was thrown entirely Ilt etit °tllbe body. Very effective, too, was histargu- "rNafinancial responsibility was divorced }4l,44.b"nainistrative responsibility .4 -a-tlac'tlt whereby the County Council will find 4:,? 11'11-4373 but it is to delegate the organisation l'"°l9sec,.4,,ttol of education to a committee. He at the Bill does nothing for secondary ArA the proper organisation of which is a 4tll.ttlg Ilational necessity. A full generation :1134t :j1"- AtalloLD implored his countrymen 1141.14 tBh-e,°tldary education in proper order, but,so tl.‘4e:f 'w`ng has been done in this direction out- The present Bill marks no ad- lltl.:,) 41 this great cause, beyond providing an thor!tY• What is the good of creating an de:it without imposing any duties upon it. this Bill,the local bodies may, if they please Ilec'nclarY education untouched, and the</p>
        <p>tile 1)? .. 1 il lit 0 1)4 t . (14 If Ila , th, 6°De tovtr l'ht i '444Attii, ‘...11 , .yr ' ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <p>1</p>
        <p>temptation to do so will be very considerable, as the cost of its proper organisation is to be thrown on the local rates, the burden of which is becom- ing heavier every year, especially in the urban districts.</p>
        <p>A similar temptation may induce a levelling down of the elementary schools. Throwing the voluntary schools on to the rates will involve a serious addition to the ratepayers' burden in both the urban and;the rural districts. The School Board rate varies in different districts, whilst in many places there is a conspiracy to keep out the School Board in order to avoid an education rate. Under this Bill, a rate will be imposed on every borough and county in the kingloic,but absolutely no pro- vision is made to rectify the incidence of the rate over the country as a whole. Instead of the educa- tion rate varying as it does from 6d. to 33. in the 2, and some districts escaping a rate altogether, education ought long ago to have been made a national charge, with a contribution, if at all, fixed and uniform, but alterable at the wish of any locality which had a special enthusiasm for education, and was willing to incur sacrifices for it. The injustice of the present system is to continue under this Bill, and in aggravated degree. Mr BRYCE said very truly that nothing was done in the Bill to unlock the door which Sectarianism has closed against Nonconforrc jets in half the schools of the country, and he showed that the Bill ignored the rights of the people, by providing that no voluntary school, so-called, should draw on the rates at the sometime that it denied public control. Sir JoHN GoasT,in his reply,evaded these points. He contented himself with arguing for the necessity of one authority for educational ',purposes and we are bound to say that he made at least a plausible case for making the County Council that authority, and not the School Board. The VICE - PRESIDENT of the Council insisted on-the import- ance of unifying local administration, and said that effective local government was impossinle without local control of finance. He employed this as an argument in favour of making the Council the education authority. It is an even more potent argument for making the public repre- sentatives supreme on the Management Com- mittee of voluntary schools. Sir JOHN GVEST never once came to close quarters with this ques- tion of public control, and its corollary, the power of appointing the teaching staff. We judge, in- deed, from his speech that he would see the volun- tary schools die without a pang so far as he is concerned. But there they are, he seems to ; say, and we must link them up somehow into our system. Well and good. Our quarrel with this Bill is as to the method under which that linking up process is to be done. Is it consonant with justice that the voluntary :schools shall be quartered on the rates, while their clerical managers are to remain supreme in con- trol? Is it not an offence against civil and religious liberty that admission to the teach- ing'stafl of half the public schools in the country should olepend on a religious test? The VICE- PRESIDENT tailed to answer these questions, and yet they are the gravamen of the case against this Education Bill. He dismissed as an airy nothing the religious difficulty, but in self-contradictory terms added apologetically that religious animosity cannot be ended by any Act of Parliament. Sir Jour does not seem to be aware that one of the chief objections to this Bill is that it intercepts religious animosities, as any Bill must which pro- poses to put elementary education at the mercy of the clergy, and to penalise Nonconformists. if the ' religious difficulty is a mere phantom, what is the meaning of the strenuous efforts of the clergy to keep the power in their own hands ? Sir JOHN GOILST says the local education authority will have full power over the secular education in voluntary schools. How can that be, when the very essence of that power—the appointment of teachers—is deliberately withheld from it? The VicE-PitEst- DENT would, we believe, make short work of the clerical pretensions if he had a free hand. He has not a free hand, unhappily, and the duty to deal with these pretensions devolves on the nation. We are convinced that; the nation does not want, and will not have clerical dictation and ascendency in the elementry schools.</p>
        <p>- - The Clerk of the Weather, we conceive, would have rather a bad time if he wore to make his appearance in any accessible locality jest now. And, truth to tell, his vagaries this year have boen enough to justify the violence which would await him. At the Crystal Palace the Australians have had the singular experience of having play stopped in May by rain, hail, and some snow; while in the Midlands snow and hail have been falling almost continuously for the hat few days. In Derbyshire the hills are covered with snow, and in some places in the valleys it is two inches deep; and in Scot- land likewise the prolongation of winter is most marked. In addition to these peculiar conditions there has been a horribly biting north-easterly wind over the North and East of the country I which has brought in:its train considerable sickness. The Coronation year, it would seam from present experiences, will not go down to posterity as the finest on record as far as the weather is concerned. The prophets do not give much comfort either, for they declare that it is not unlikely that the pres- ent unpleasant conditions will extend throughout the whole of this month, relief only coming a few weeks before the Coronation. The prospect is not alluring, therefore ; and it is not surprising that many people are delaying their return co England until Emma chaage for the better has taken place. The only people who are likely to benefit by the unusual weather are the doctors, whose hearts, no doubt, are gladdened by the number of colds the nor'-easter has succeeded in manufacturing, in conjunction with the leaving off of overcoats , and winter wraps.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</p>
        <p>A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>It may not he out of plsce to remind the reader that this is the Merry Month of May, the season for mirth and jollity, when shepherd boys sing mad- rigals, and all the world is gay. But during the week there has been snow in many places,with hail, sleet, and thunierstorms in others ; and in some parts of the Country ponds have been covered with of ice, while at Yarmouth a man has in the street from cold. But we are not sup- daiethdin coating posed—at any rate by the Spring poet "—to let details like these divert our minds from the all- important circumstance that we have reached the " sweet of the year. the " idyllic season." in brief the "poet's month," and all the rest of the delusive 1 phrases Which miserable mortals write between their sneezings and wheezings as they crouch over a May fire. --- It will be acceptable news to many householders in the provinces just now, that the Government are thinking of removing the embargo which prohibits the importation of cattle from South America. For, in the presence of increased and increasing prices of meat, due no doubt to other causes as well as the great Beef Trust, any such step as this which can be safely adopted would mitigate a kind of hardship that certainly tells upon families the number of which does not always correspond with the income.</p>
        <p>The British farmer has now something real, and very tangible, to exercise his faculty for grumbling</p>
        <p>••••••</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>EXAMIN_gii t•_RII 0A Y MAY 9, 196</head>
        <p>THE EXAMIN_gii t•_RII 0A Y MAY 9, 196</p>
        <p>upon Indeed, a decision Oven this week by a Court composed of the Lord Chief Justice and two other Judges may well strike dismay into his heart. Hitherto the dairy farmer has had nothing to fear, however poor the quality of his milk, so long as he Could show to the satisfaction of the magiairates that he had not tampered with it by depriving it of part of its cream, or impoverishing it by means of water. But henceforth, apparently, it will be no defence to a charge, to prove vac the milk is just as it came from the cow. An t.ppeal had been lodged against the decision of a bench of magis- trates who had convicted a milk dealer of selling milk which he had purchased from a farmer, the said milk not containing the statutory quantity of fat. It vvas not urged that the milk had been adulterated,but the cows were alleged to be treated in such a way that their lacteal tribute to the pail was not of the quality which might be ell:lac:led in ordinary circumatances. The Lord Chief Justice and Mr Justice Channell were agreed in tninking the conviction must be affirmed, inasmuch as the milk was not of the nature, substance, and quality demanded. But Mr Justice Darling dissented, on the unctmllenged tact that the purchaser asked for new milk and got it. It was new, because .it was supplied the same day from the cow, and it was milk, because it was supplied as it came from the cow. Ano yet the judical decision resolved itself into an affirmation et the previous conviction,with- out costs.</p>
        <p>In every nook and corner of the .provinces, one of the thoughts that are uppermost in most men's minds, and will continue to be so for several more days, is that of what will be the final result of the momentous meeting at Vereeniging on Thursday next. The very name of the place is of good omen Vereeniging is the frontier station on the Vaal' River where the railway systems of the Transvaal and the Orange River Colony come together, arid the name of the township signifies Union. Hence, nothing could be more appropriate than that at the final ballot of the Boers on Thursday, the founda- tions should then and there he laid of a lasting union between all the white races in South Africa. Anyhow, heads of families, and some of the younger members, will be interested in knowing that pros cut portents are distinctly encouraging. The facts that De Wet has been brought to recognise he futility of further resistance and that Dalarey is content to be guided by the decision of the majority must count for much in the minis of wavering burghers. It will be in strict accord with a pecu- liar trait in the Boer character that there shoulo be some haggling as the outcome of the Vereenig ing meeting; but when Once the patent fact is brought home to the commandos that independ- ence hes gone for ever, the rest will be easy. and while adhering to the main points of Lord Kitchener's proclamation, Great Britain can afford to be generous in regard to matters that are non- essential. The general feeling in the best informed quarters now appears to be that, irrespective ol the" peace movement," hostilities in South Africa will be over before the day fixed upon for the Conflation.</p>
        <p>Poor-law Guardians of the Evesham Union hav- ing come to an agreement to pay their medical officer 7s 61 per daily inspection of all the casual arriving at the Workhouse, the Local Government Board have now signified their approval, Tons it is regarded as next to impossible that any infected tramp who may seek the hospitality of the estab- lishment there would afterwards continue his peril- ous wanderings. As soon as the disease is detected strict isolation will be enforced so as to prevent its further dissemination. Other Unions may, with advantage to themselves and to the community at large, adopt the course decided upon at Evesham. since there is abundant evidence to show that tramps frequently convey small pox into locolities previously uninfected; and as for the victims them- selves, they are so reckle,s and so ignorant or in- different as to the consequences to others.that they neither know nor care to know the nature of the malady from which they are suffering. They are sometimes apt, indeed, to attribute the mark.' on their skin to flea-bites, or to some rash which it is worth nobody'e trouble to take any notice of. And that is how the disease is liable to spread.</p>
        <p>Cardiff Town Council having decided not to run 'Sunday electric carp, the Cithays Liberal Cub passed a resolution condemning such a step and have elicited the warm approval of the leadiug Tory journal for so doing. The Club, say + the newspaper in question, consists chiefly of working men, persons who know their own needs and those of the working community in Cardiff; adding that it will "be well for every working men's club, and every body of working men to record a protest against the Town C4uncil's action." On that body, the writer " fears that there is lit•le sympathy with the knights of labour"</p>
        <p>It would appear that to the arrangement for a Penny Post between Great Britain and Australir, as mentioned in last week's letter, the home oepaq- ment continues to show a certain amount of opposi- tion. If the present Government had been in power at the time of Rowland Hill's proposal for a penny postage in this country. it is fair to infer that it would have held out against the innovation. But what was inevitable then is inevitable now, or something very much like it. The general feel- ing is that the concession has been made in order to signalise the Coronation year, just as Canada established an ocean penny post to mark the year of Queen Victoria's diamond jubilee, and it will be extremely unfortunate if a deadlock should occur on the question as between the Motherland and the Commonwealth of Australia at so imp ,rtaot a period of Imperial history. Yet the answer given in the House of Commons the other evening, by Mr Austen Chamberlain to Sir John Leng, has not ten- ded to remove the uncertainty which existed. It would be deplorable if any trivial difficulty, either of business or of sentiment, should be allowed to interfere with the inter-Imperial penny post irlAal. The hope is, therefore, entertained that all the elements of deadlock may be removed by mutual consent and consideration before next month has dawned. to the crowning ceremonial of which period all true Imperialists eagerly look forward.</p>
        <p>May is the month for Xhibit ins, and this week two of these entarprise4 Levu attracting large multitudes of provincials Neither the exhibition at Wolverhampton nor at Cork, h iwever, is on te scale of the Glasgow venture of last year. Yet each of these exhibitions will fulfil a function that is re- creative and instructional. Wolverhampton is, of Course,the Mecca of the Midlands. It draws upon a densely populated district, and should prove a financial success. The exhibition at Cork, where the idea has been turned to excellent account on more than one occasion, attracts large bodies of tourists to one of the most convenient centres for the fascinating vistas of Southern Treland and Kill- arney. It would be well if Ireland were much more than it is the happy bunting ground of pro- vincial England in particular and of British tourists generally. The holiday maker can certainly enjoy within easy distance ol C,)rk the scenery for which it would be difficult to find a parallel in any other spot on the planet's surface. It is, in reality, pass- ing strange that in this Coronation year there has not been any first-class exhibition organised in London. No doubt the element of insufficient time can be pleaded in extenuation, but it seems quite time that London, like Chicago, Paris, and Glasgow, should revive some of those splendid traditions of the past in this resp,,et.</p>
        <p>2/6 PIAPEritAL 41.JARt (B ' rrn.,pB tbiCLUDED.) SCOTCH AS 11 IttlsH WHISKNIY. ALeO AT 3/-. 3/9 4/6 A.ND 5/3 PER, QUART, 1/8,2/-, 2/6, 3/-, AND 3/0 rßa BCC/7LE, FUL, CON BROS WINH VI/WM ANTS, 26, tth'NSERW sed,go;,., LIVOPO (2 ,lionto4 fro n 'entral and Lime Street ,tAtionc)</p>
        <p>FUNERAL OF THE LAte MR. R.</p>
        <p>PEACOCK, FIUNCO'FC''•!.</p>
        <p>On Sunday afternoon at the Runeord deraetery, the remains of the late Mr Ralph Petcock Were-laid in their last ',rein,' place. The deccmSed gentlema'n tor many years had been connected wi h the ;own in a' tfemi-publie capacity and w s inost widely keown and teepee...ed. For the gr ater rare of tee life he was employed by the Britgewa er Naviget ion Company and afmr an arduous life of tell, retired some two years ago. For forte -.seven years Mr P eacock had been a prominent i.y preacher in connection wi.n toe Primitive Meteci list b ,dy. and had done yeoman seroic . for the c nine :don, Fits discourses were ma k d with originality and lucidity, and he riveted the attention of his hi arms upon any sub- j.ct he tree. ed. Some time ago he was presented with a lone service rreelai by the proprietors of a well-kiOWII rellei,ll+ pipAr for nis religious work. He was . a z lalms P S.A. mau i rid help,4l forwar i the movement in Rum. Corn considerable. H • was a devoted angler and through this soar may be traced the cause of his death for in handling finale fishing tackle he lacerated his hand. Blood-poisoning int corned with fatal reeults. Though he had arteined ,he green old age of seventy—seven di- ceased was hale and hearty up to the time of his death. The funeral on Sunday was largolv attended by repra- sentaliVo9 of the religious body with which the deceased had been identified ,11 his life, and members of the P B.A Prior to leasing !he deneasefs late residence in Ashridge-street, the friends gathered round the coffin, wiech was placed on chairs in the street, and sang " We Ishall meet beyond lee river." The procession at length moved off in the following order :—R •presentetiven of the Primitive Methodi.t body, &amp;e. ; Rev W A Eyre (superintendent of the Presten Brook circuit). tifeslrs J Rowland, R linden, 8 R iveland, J Colton, P Reyle, J I Fleet, F Coker, fl Aekerley, WFI ixsvorth, S Bcyers, , James Stubbs, W Shaw (seamen's rnis,onarvi 11 Stubb+, S PArkinson, W El Richardson, S Stubbs, T E rkham, R Rudd, T Seel°. John Fleet. rem., R J Illidge, H Weod- ' ward, J Ba.ker. W Clarke Riamsentetives of the P S A..—Me.srs F Beck. W Handley, F Littler (secre- tary). T A Faulkner, George Randles, Jams M-ircer, W ETV'S, C 1339 t,., C Wilson, E Mellor, E Teylor, H C irk r, T Riley, T Clucas, J R Scram El Nell. F Guy, G Ruler, Joseph Parker, Edward Bolen, P Fl.rri,on, II Rowe, Robert Lowe, T 1 Anderrion, R Marsh, J Fl Miller (Widnes), J Elayes, and J B.zley Next came the hearse and bearers, viz., Messrs C Hill, E Aeron, J Sityle, and P J lilidge. The mourners, whe brought up the rear,were :—M.ssrs G El Peacock (sen). E Care, J and D Dimon, G Rowlands and J Weterwn ti (sons-io-law,) T Clare, J Wtterworth, W Gteigh, G ‘Vilcork, and J Heaton (grandsons), E Roach and W Peacock (nephews), and other friends Tire route taken was Egerton-street, Devenshire-scietare, and Greenway-roed, to the Primi•ive Methedist, Chapel, where a short service was conducted by the Rev W A Eyre. Prayer was off, -ed by Mr W Shaw, and several of the decoaaed's fey° irite hymes were sung, the pro- ceedings tying alt oge -her if am impressive character. The precession afterwards re-formed, and moved on to the cemetery where the final scene was enacted amid the singing of other favourite hymns of the deceased. A large crowd gathered in toe vicinity ef the grave and as the remains were,cemmitted to the earth not a few were visibly affected.</p>
