      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>CHARGE AGAINST A WIDNES PUBLICAN.</head>
        <p>CHARGE AGAINST A WIDNES PUBLICAN.</p>
        <p>Sequel to the Cup Final at Runcorn.</p>
        <p>At the Widnes Petty Sessions, on Ti-unclay before the Mayor (Alderman W W G4/age), an S Aldermal G J N4l, Mr James Fiswick, licensee of the Waterloo \rants Waterloo-road, Widnes, was charged with refusing to P ic-c sN.hle Bretherton to his licensed premises on Tuesday, April 29. Mr Knowles defended.—Policc- constable Brerherron stated that at ten minutes past eleven he saw defendant and two other men eo into the side door. Witness followed them, and knocked at the door. After knocking fr a trial-ter of an hour two girls answered him frun the window. Witness asked for the landlord, and the girls replied, " Oh, you can- not see Mr Fish wick to-night, as he has gone to bed." dhortly after the I ndlord came to the window, and it 97/1.9 alleg,d that he said. "If you want to come int-m my house you mu-t Come at six o'clock in the morning." Polio,arinstable Dickson corroborated.</p>
        <p>James Fishwick, licensee, said be had been in the I place s-conteen years. He had been over to Runcorn to see a footb,ill rn.tch,and came home wiPla two friends. rhey left him at the corner of his house,and went home. Witness went to attend to some cash in the drawor,and then turned out the lights and went to bed, He strongly denied that he refused the police admittance, saying that if they went to bring someone else, meaning the sergeant, they could come and search the premises. Patrick Quinn, a well-known billiard player and a chemical foreman, stated that he came home with the defendant, but did not go inside his house, Superintendent Keeley : We are not charging you with having been on the premises; we are charging you with refusing the police admittance. Mr Knowles : Then I will not call Drory...</p>
        <p>Hugh Moran said that on the night in question he saw a policeman kicking at the door, and later saw two girls come to an upstairs window. Podoe-constable Brether- ton told them that be wanted to see the proprietor of the establishment. One replied that he could not see him ; chat he was in bed. The land- lord then came to the window when Brother- ton again Eaid, "We want to search the house' as we believe some one is in. Mr Fishwick replied that it was not nen,ssary, as there was only his family presPnt in the house. If they wanted to search they , could go and fetch their superior officer, and they wero welcome to do so. _ _</p>
        <p>Margaret and Mary Whittle were next called, and corroborated the former evidence. This concluded the evidence for the defence, aad the bench dismissed the Case.</p>
      </div>
