      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES</p>
        <p>PROMOTION FOR A O,I3OICORN CAPTAIN.</p>
        <p>Our readers will be plea-kai to learn r hat Captain Young of tne Salvation Army has been promoted to the 2nd Corp, -tationed at Manchester. Yet all will be sorry to learn of his departure, for during the twelve m niche Captain Young has laboured in the town, he has proved him-elf a hard- working, energetic, yet genial individual Arno has gained the goodwill of all with whom he has come in contact. During his stay in the town the aggressive work of the army has greatly increased in vigour, conversions have been numer- ous, and on the whole; Captain Young is to be con- gratulated on the success which he has so worthily achieved. His farewell sermon was delivered on Sunday before a crowded congregation. Captain Young will carry with him into his mission work at Mamihester he good wishes of every section of the community. Si' JOHN AMBULANCE. The annual meeting of ,he Ku lc 're branch of St John Ambulance As tociation wash -Id at the Tech- nical [natant., on Tuesiday evening under the presi- dency of Mr F W Ilutton. Tne balance aheel,,which has alr-arly been published, was pre,entel by Mr Baukes. It showed an income of £5 1.9 s 81, whicn after payment of eKpensee left a balanc- in hand of .£4 17s 21. Th., Secretary, Mr Gregory, p, esented his report, from which it appeared that 26 had passed the fir-.r , and the final examinatione. These names have alrealy applared in our columns. The election of .dEters was ?nen proceed. d with. Four gentlemen were nominated for the presidency namely Me 98,8 F J Norman, It El Posne-t, H Con- greve, and J Salk-Id. On a ballat being taken Mr F J Norman topped the poll. Messrs Gregory and Bankes were re-appointed secretary and treasurer resuecively The certificates and medals were afterwards distributed to the success- ful tud nts.</p>
        <p>PRESENTATION TO MR JOHN MEADOIVertorT. On Sa urdey evenin2 at the office of tile Upper Mersey Navigation Commissioners. Runcorn, Mr John Meadowcrott was presented with a losauti• t,:fully ihutninated address on his retirement from the office of tras'ee ot the Duke's Club. Mr James Rankers presided over the meeting, and b-sides Mr Msadowcroft there were present :—Mr 4 Meadow- croft, Mr and Mrs J K Digges. Mr and Mrs ILdward Meadowcroit, Master Stanley Mead,owcroft, Mrs Robinson, Messrs T Simister, W Rawlinson, W IloX- w.;rth R W owen (treasurer of the club), Geo. Rollingworth (se-retary), R Hughes, J H Miller, It Fazackerley,ll Crompton, it Role, J Rose, W Miller, A Cioutih, J iloxworth, P Parr, T Royle, and others. Mr lioxworth made the presentation an I a number of speeches were delivered. The following is the text of the address which was beautifully engrossed and enclosed in a handsome frame : —" June 30th, 1901. Presented to John Meadow:croft, esq., by the trustees, officials, and members of the Duke's Club, Runcorn, as a slight recognition of his ungrudging and valuable services as trustee, during the past 24 years. They are convinced of the great benefits conferred upon the members by his connection with the club, and they ask Mr Meadowcrof ac- ceptance of this humble tribute of appreciation, wherein they have tried to give expression to their : respect and esteem, although feeling that no words of theirs can adequarely express their gratitude for the valuitble supp nt that he ha: always affor:ed them ; and they ,rusit that although unfortunately no longer a trustee he raay long be able to extend towards them that kindly support and interest that have been of such h-n fir '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>RUN 11 04,,1E5.</head>
        <p>RUN 11 04,,1E5.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDRNT.I</p>
        <p>Judging from the tone of the discussion on Toes- nay. either the Rural Council has not finished with Mr. Pemberton or Mr. Pemberton has not finished with the Council. Many members now think that tr..Pemberton is an man, while others think the reverse. It is dear that the matter is by no means yet settled, and it is equally clear that the Council intend to give this " much abused gentleman" a lair hearing as to the merits and demetits of his complaint. A spacial Committee has been appoimed to meet this recalcitraut.house- builder, amid there is every probability that the dispute will be amicably arranged without it being taken to a higher court.</p>
        <p>The Guardisns and the C uneil were each engaged in pursuinz the " trivial round, the zommon task of routine business, nothing occurring worthy of special mention.</p>
        <p>Though it is ping tabs a great day in Runcorn on Coronatim day, and though the committee appointed have met to consider the made ot celebration, filching elk cis' has yet been communicated t ) the puolic. It has leaked out, however, that there was a battle royal between the Anglicans and the Dissenters. The Sunday schools procession will have to be headed by some school—even it it be "!.1r Jones' Mission. This pit of honour is clveted by all. Toe Pari-h Clutch claim they shluld head the proces- sion becan-e their's is the oldest church in the town while the Dissenters thought it wat the best coarse to ballit for position. Ater a heated discussion : the committee resolved t 3 settle ..,ne pri3rity of position by ballot.</p>
        <p>-- The weather is causing univ-irssi dissatisfaction. Insteal of May sunshine we are being treated to March wind. and April ,howers. Atcogether it is atalut ai unp!ea,ant, for the time of the year as it could posnbly be. Jr is to be hoped we shall have more genial weather for Mrnit.uatide and tU Cor- onation festivitieQ.</p>
        <p>Drowning Fatality at Bewsey Locks.—A Widnes • Man's Sad End. _ _</p>
        <p>_ A sad drowning fatality occurred at Bewsey Locks, Warrington, on We Inesday morning. It appears that a man named Walter Harrisoa, living at No 1, Dock-cottages, on the Saakey and St Helens Canal, Widnes, was sent to bring an ice boar,, the property of the London and North- Western Railway Company, that was lying at Winwick, down the canal, in company with a man named John Sran, on &amp;bout a quarter past ten they were at B-,wsey Locks Stantou was towing the boat and Herrison was steering. When the beat was in the locks Harrison got off and went to open the lack gate. In order to do 60 he had to descend to a stone platform somewhat below the level of the quay wall. In order to reach this he jumped and fell into the water. 6tanton on the other side heiring a splash ran and tarew a life- buoy that was hung at the point to the man, but he did not rise after the fist imrnersioa With the help of a labourer named William Bate, the body was ecovered with the lock rake within a short time but life was extinct. 'the body was placed on the boat and so removed to Widnes, where the [facts were reported to the phlice and to the coroner.</p>
        <p>1.0. G T. HOPE OF bABLESTOWN LODGE 1644. The weekly meeting of tne above was held in War- grave Hall, on Monday. The Lodge deputy read the G C 'remplare quarterly circular and also Rave notice of Convention at Ashton on Siturday May 10th. The Secretary's report for the quarter showed that nine new members had been initiated during the quarter, and seven readmitted as members. The tollowing were elected officers for the ensuing quarter, C T Mc, Cutler ; V T Sister L Woods ; Secretary Bro T Greening; Assistant Secretary, I Sister Miss Ord ; Final Secretary, Bro Wakefield ; Treasurer, Bro J Warburton ; Chaplain, Sister I,firs Haiti ; l rsal, Bro Hatton ; Deputy Marshal er Rovvi 1. Bro Tunicliffe ; P C.T. Bro, .vere passed, showing a good bhihnco-</p>
