      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>MUNICIPAL TRADING.</head>
        <p>MUNICIPAL TRADING.</p>
        <p>The Town Council and the Sealing of Tenders. ---- Standing Order again Altered.</p>
        <p>It will be remembered that a short time ago, on the prooosition of Councillor Bennett,the Warrington Town Council amended s'anding grder No 34., so as to provide that all tenders suomitted for Corporation work should be enclosed in envel ipu,s sealed with wax. At the close of he ordinary business at the Council meeting on Tues- day evening, Councillor Tinnion brought forward a resolution " That standing order No 34. be altered by the substitution of the words " securt lc fastened" for the words "sealed with wax," wnere they occur in line nine of such crier." In doing so, Councillor Tinnion remarked that his only reason for moving the alteration was because he thought if the words" seourely fastened " were substi- tuted for "sealed in wax " the standing order would be more in the spirit and intention of the original resolution moved by Councillor Bennett. As a matter of fact, it nad b ea found that when some envelopes had been sealed with wax they were not as securely fastened as they would have been if they had simply been gummed in the ordinary way. If the standing order was again amended, it would not prevent aayone from sealing their tenders with war, but the envelopes must be securely fastened. If he had thought the altera- tion would in any way defeat the object which Coun- cillor B.mnett had in view wli.,u WI moved his resolution he 6bouli not have proposed it. Elia only object was to strengthen the standing order, It did not matter how the envelopes were sealed but they must be securely fastened when they came into the hands of the com- mittees. - -</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON seconded. Councillor BENNETT opposed the resolution. He quite admitted the Nina fides of Councillor Tinnion., He also admtted that even sealing wax was not an absolutely impregnable defence and it might be possible to tamper with the tenders but people ' usually regarded sealing wax as boing more secure than tee ordinary gummed flap. He did s# himself, and , although sealing was was not ideal be thought it was better than guru. He did not believe the allegations which had been made against Corporation officials and others, but he wished. the Corporation to be absolutely like Cresat's wife—above reproach. If they could get out of the atmomhere of suspicion and unfair sugges• tions which was banging about the Corporathn he thought they were justified in adhering to the standing. order. Alderman MONICS pointed out that there was now an instrument sold which made it absolutely impossible to , open toe envelope without detection. He suggested that the standing order showd provide that persons tender- ing should use this instrument. Alderman Sorrow facetiously suggested that as Councilor Bennett was always so particular about these matters, he should add that it be necessary in putting wax on the envelopes to have a witness who would declare on the back of the envelops that it had been properly waxed and delivered. (Laughter). That would perhaps meet all the difficulties. He thought it was most ridiculous if a tender were fastened in the usual and proper way that it s'...ould uot b 3 sufficient for all purposes. COuncillor livtirorr said the Mayor would be able to bear him out when he said that at a recent clinmittee meeting, tenders were received which though seal( d with wax were not recurelv fasPene.i. Ile naentioneci the matter at the time and the M:yor a:rreed that an alteration like that proposed was advisable.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR Raid that havwg been on all the com- mittees and seen a vast number of tenders opened be had seen in.tances in which the dab of wax was n) pro-</p>
        <p>tection whatever, and a groat rnaay envelopes were far less securely fastened when they hal wax than when they had tie ordinary gummed flap. The members of the Council %Vora all anxilu9 to do the same thing. Their only object was how best to accomplish their purpose. 'roe resolution was carriel Councillor Bennett being the only dissentient.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>GOLBORNE MAY QUEEN FESTIVITIES.</head>
        <p>GOLBORNE MAY QUEEN FESTIVITIES.</p>
        <p>On Satur lay the annual celebration by the ichabl- tants of Go;borne of the incoming of the " Morrie " mouth of May took place. The morning opened with a stoady downpour of rain bat Jul,: before noon, ," Old Sot" made a welcome apoearanco and he raised the hopes of the committee by smiling in unbroken suc- cession all through the afternoon. The children assembled on the Reid at half-past one, and were speedily got int) order by the marshals, Messrs John Caunce and James Halsall, so that the commencement of the tour round the village was not unnecessarily delayed. The procession was appropriately headed by Corporal J Rill and Lance Corrrals L Ramsey and W Boardman, a contingent of the 32ad Company Imperial Yeomanry, who had been out on active service in South Africa. Tue three were dressed in khaki and wore their war medals. The Golb arne Band came next, under the conductorship of Mr D Pimblett, and then followed morris dancers, flower girls, bakers, rising generation, hospital nurses, and footballers, and the *multitudinous characters which one always finds in May Day processions. The choice for Queen this year fell upon Miss Christina Glover of Brock-street, who had only attained the age of 13 years, and the recipient of the mucb-eoveted honour bore herself with true queenly grace. She was attind in a dress of heliotrope coloured material, with a wreath and a beautiful bouquet of fern and whit., flowers, whilst the carriage in which she was seated was drawn by the customary four grey horses. Meanwhile the stewards at the various entrances had been busy admitting people to the grounds, and it is estimated that between five and six thousand people had assembled. The procession, on arriving at the ground,</p>
        <p>Itook up its position in the ring, and after a slight bait again marched round the enclosure. The old.. , fashioned May pole dance, which never seems to lose popularity, was executed by the dancers Who tripped along merrily to the strains of , the Golbc roe Band, and the scene was rrikingly effec- tive, the atsembly testifying their appreciation by at veritable salvo ot applause, Griffis and Reface comedy trick cyclists, gave an acceptable turn, the music being' ,I contributed by the Eariestvwn Band. The shirr dance, I by the chddren, who proved adepts, was also applauded, iand the Coronation clog dance, which was ex-cuted in saurt style, aiso received recognition. The ea-Queen. Miss Ashton, then took the crown off the bearers and placed it on the Queen's bead in a graceful manner. The Golborne Band supplied the music for this important item and also this bell dance, which was admirably per- formed, AWr the striking tableau " nritain," the crowd assembled on the green, and dancing was kept up until dusk. attier attractions were also provided in the shape of merry-go-rounds, auut sallies, swinging boats, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>The directors of management, to whom great praim is due, were Messrs E Last (chairman), John Wakefluld I (aecretarv), J Wakefield (jun.), J Caunce, W Ern, Eiward P.ogers, T Ashcroft, 'l' II Cooke, and James Halsall. The admirab:e manner in which the ceremonies on the field wFre carried out was due to the Ir3.C.'s, Messrs J T Molyneux, sod Cartwright. In addition to tLens Mr John Wakefield (jun.), made a very efficient captain of the fiAd. (be teachers of the dancers, Miss A Bullock and Mr J Thompson, are to be congratulated upon the sloe:lent show made by their pupils, whilst the excellent order in which the proces.icni moved was due to the efforts of Mesq's .1 Caunce and J H.lsal, the marshals. The committee had two very able officia!s its Messrs J a Dearden (treasurer), and W Batt(r,by (secre— tary), who both had a share in the more onerous duties coanected with the annual event.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <p>-</p>
        <p>NOTES BY THE WAY AT EARLESTOWN•</p>
        <p>RILESPONDE,</p>
        <p>[By A Con— The members of the Maker&amp;ld Amateur Draraa— tic Society have amongst them, the making of a very successful society. The ineces which they submitted last week in the Town Hal!, Were of the light order, but they were such as commended themselves to the popular taste.. The artistes have the satisfaction of knowing that they devoted their time and talents in a worthy and very laudable cause, and although a section of the public may not see fit to extsnd their practical sympathy to the object, it nevertheless remains a fact that a place like Earlestown, abounding in risky trades, should certainly not be without a hospitall There is a+ certain amount of energy on the part of the pro— moters, which will doubtless in time bear the fruit it deserves.. Workers for a hospital cannot certainly be accused of selfishnesN, because the establishment. of such an institution will be of the greatest good to the greatest number.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the hospital Committee was hehl. on Tuesday night and it was decided to push on with the forthcoming bazaar, which is to be held in the open air, in July next.. La connection with the bazar it is in contempla- tion to have a demonstration. and it i hoped the tradespeople, and others will be generous enough to help. If a good round slim can be raised by the bazaar, it will do more to stimulate the laggards than any amount of meeting or explaining. Pro- perly worked the bazaar.by reason ot its being help in the open air, should be the most important event of the year, and £5OO should he easily raised. The Police have raised nearly sufficient funds for their ambulance, but of what use is an ambulance with- out a hospital. To my mind one is contingsnt upon the other.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>CRICKET.</head>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>Earlestown have started their season very well so far having suffered defeat in the two matches, they have prayed. B3th the clubs they have met are -old rivals and honours are pretty evenly divided. Eerlestown however miss their principal bowler, Alderson who is at present playing at Ox - ford, and probably there may be an alteration when he return-. The second team managed to give Leigh a severe drubbing on Saturday.</p>
        <p>Newton are doing fairly well although they have not the assistance of Huish, tbeir professional. On Saturday they defeated WallB7-4 on the home ground, but the second team were beaten at Wal— lasey</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN &amp; NEWTON.</p>
        <p>POLICE CA SM.</p>
        <p>At the Earlestown police station on Monday. before Mr J W Emmett, John S•anton, farm labourer, was charged with being drunk and dis- orderly and fined 2s 63 and costs. At the Newton Police Court on Monday morning before Mr T J Gillespie, John Thoro and Thomas Brannon were charged with being drunk and dis- orderly in Winwick on Sunday night, and were ° ed 5e each, including costs. zal PERSISTENT CRUELTY TO A WIFE.</p>
        <p>A'', the Newton Police Court. on Thursday. before Mr T J Gillespie and Dr Dowling, James Baker, a- machinist, residing at Vulcan Village, Earlestown, was charged with persistent cruelty to his wife, Sarah. Mr A E White, who prosecuted, said de- fendant had not worked for six weeks. On the 23rd ult, defendant pulled his shoes off his feet and ent them to pawn, then began throwing p-ts at omplainaut, and drove her out. On complainant returning defendant dragged her out of the house by the hair of her head, placed her against a wall, and commenced to thump her violently in the tace and head, causing a black eye as well as 1u7131:14 and bruises on the head. Complainant, who was a. school cleaner, had had to leave defendant, who had chastised her and her daughter as being too lazy to keep him.--This atatem-nt was borne out by evidence, complainant stating that a sum of money she came into possession of eight years ego had been expended on keeping defendant.—Delendant was fined 103 and costs for the assault, and ordered to pay 103 a waek towards complainant's mainten- ance.</p>
        <p>--- A HAYDOCK RETURNED SOLDIER IN TROUBLE'.</p>
        <p>Henry Chisnall, of Haydock, who said he had been at the war in South Africa for eighteen months, was charged at Newton Police Court on Thursdsy with be.ing drunk and assaulting Po'ice constable Steele, in Haydock, on April 26th The constable said that prisoner was mad drunk when he came upon him. Prisoner vio- lently kicked him on the legs several times, threw him on the ground, kicked a piece off the top of his ear, injured hie wrist, and did other injuries to him. Prisoner explained that be met a returned- Yeoman in St Helens, and they had several drinks together. He knew nothing of the a-sault.—The Chairman of the maci3trates said they were deter- mined to put that sort ot bullying down. Prisoner was fined 2s 6d and costs for being drunk and 10a and costs for the assault on the police.</p>
        <p>AKRRFIELD AMATEUR DRAMATIC SOCIRTY. There was only a meagre audience on Friday night when this Society reproduced " All that glitters is not gold." There was no deterioration whatever in the representation, but if anything an improvement, each of the artiKes doing their best to excel, and their eff,irts. met with well-merited applause, Miss Corlett, as " Martha Gibbs " was perfect, and Miss Newton as Lady Valeria acted with charming grace. On Satudav night " Paul Pry" was staged to a full house, and the merriment was well sustahael. The Oaracters infused plenty of life and action in their parts, and the applause was spontaneous and. Renuine. It would not be wise to single out any character for special men- tion, where all did their best and strained every effort to please. The piece went with a swing throughout and there was not a single hitch. The comments of the audiencee were entirely favourable, and encourag- ing to the Society t.) secure further successes. On Saturday afternoon, a miscellaneous entertainment Was given to the cbild7en an,:l the hall was filled. The programme was under the charge of Mr H Jones of Newton, who succeeded in fully pleasing the youngsters. The items Nvre " Here comes the gipsies " by the Primitive Ladies' Choir; song " The fortune teller " Miss Stronach ; flag drill by boys of Congregational school; trio " Three old maids of Leer Misses Stronach,Woods and Baxter ;humorous sketch, Mr P Glynn; song and flag drill "The death: less army " ; trio " Three Quakeresses," Misse Stronach, Toombs and Baxter; bayonet exercise, boys ; Ladies' choir ""The fairest day.'</p>
