        <head>LATE MR. JOHN PLAIT.</head>
        <p>THE LATE MR. JOHN PLAIT.</p>
        <p>The TowN CLERIC r.ad a l-t er horn Mrs Platt, thank. lag th+- CoAdel! tor Zheir kind expressions in regard t 3 15. r late husn-ind and tor their sympathy and condol- ence with her Li her b.reavetnent.</p>
        <p>THE NEW BUILDINGS. SUGGESTED MUNICIPAL OFFIC;ES.</p>
        <p>At a meetwg oi the Watch Committee held on the 10:h April a report was read from the Borough Surveyor to the effect. that the new police premises were to nearly comple ad that they could at any time be occupied for polico purposes, and it was rem Avec' tilt the attention of the architects be called to the advisability of com- pleting the premises as 8)00 as posdble. It was also resolved that wood block flooring be substituted for asphdt paving in the parade room ; that the B nouga Surveyor obtain indication boards and have names of he different rooms painted on the doors : and that the cinstand iii R?lands-stree• be dispensed with, and that e cabstand be appuimed in Bridge-street opposite the gable sad of the Royal Court Hotel. The MAYOR e, op 14pd the adoption of the naiiautee.—</p>
        <p>Toe DEPUTY MAYOR second, d. COLIRCiIIOY SMETHUR T drew attention to the ncom- mendatma to sub.:nuts wcod block flooring for aaphabi paving in the parade room. He said ths Chairman of the Finance Committee explained to the Council at the last meeting that this Cmarnittee had already neerapsnt itself to the extent of something lk 44.000. fl thought it was quite tine the Cornauttee g tve up alter- ing th,,ir plan-) and running them into op..cial extras of this kind.</p>
        <p>Council or TnlsmN said they were con'inually changing their rnidds in regard to these pr-mises. A considerable turn had been spent, considerably more certainly than was intended at the outset, and they should really be very careful lo,fore they spent any more. lle had rissn to get an opportunity of having some explanation in re- forence to the indication boards witli the nanuPs of tOue d,fferent rooms painted upon tiv,m, because he had ' heard a goad deal of criticism from the trceplyerß, who were entitled to criticize, as regarded the amount of room in the buildings. It was ass rtPd that there was considerably more room than there was any apparent necessity for, that was tor police purposes. It had been suggested that there was suffie,ient room there to spare to accommodate the whole of the clerical staff of the . Corporation. Whether that was so or not they should have culls-irk •tively settled by the committee. le proposed,therefore. that the resolution be referred back, so that they might have a joint report from the li)rough surveyor and Chief C onstable as to the amount of space which would be required for police purposes, anl airtietti“r it would not be possible to accommodate some potion or probably the whole of the clerical staff of the C Tiniest, a in the buildinae Couac:llor BEsNurr said the proposal was a reasonable one and be seconded it. It simply aaked for thorowb report as to the accommodation and whether the roorais could be adapted to other Corporation purposes. It they could they ought to hp. The Expenditure on thee buildings had been enormous and ont of proportion to the requirements of the police station. Eli would lap to ask when it was likely the old iie station would be finally closed and prisoners transferred to the new pre- rnisti,; ? The pr,,sent condition of things must be a ,eriocs, and he thought an unialcessary expense. He would like an assurance that Every possible step was being taken to accelerate matters in this direction.</p>
        <p>Alderman SUTTON isain he never objected to any informa-lon being given to the Council in reference to all :mat!ers on the ageada and e oecially inf regard to maters which core likely to cost very much, but he would like to pAnt out that the suggested alteration of these premises would involve a large cost inasmuch as they were at present fitted up for the accommodelon and colvonience of the police officials. Although they might have morelmom I.iao they at present revired be did not sea how thec cmu ut:l'r it for anything differ- ent from what was nec•smary fu• the purpo es of the pllicr. fie had, however, no objection to the report,</p>
        <p>CotrieillOr SMETHErit9T drew a,tectiori to thra fact that b. hid suggested this matter to the Council two aimathg</p>
        <p>Tho DRYLITY MAYOR said he had hpard soom curious amend-nen7s in the Council, but to use a somewhat common savine this one took toe bl4cut. (('aughter.) It was tla vt the Council should critiolze, what its own , action ameunted to not many months ago. The Wats% committee of that date, of which then he had not the honour io be a member, after years of careful delibera- tion prepared instruc'ions which were submitted to the archi tsplls and up In those instructions, in co-npetition, a certain set of Dials was accepted by ihe Council after the plans had been on public exhibitiol for a fortnight. Thole builditve had been erected for police purpo.ces. T ey had not been ercc'ed for municipal offices and it was Absurd and childish to begin and try to do the whole work over aam. They had committed them- selves to the contract, the plans and the architect's sug- gestions and he would like to know when they were gain°, to finien the work. The resolution open which this discussion had arisen was a perfectly simple one and c..pable of b,ing understood by anyone. It simply pro- vided for a board somewhere in the build-1 , jog indicating where the various rooms were and for painting the names on the doors and ,it was arranged for originally in the plans. It seemed to him that the amendment was very silly and rtfl,cted upon the intelligence and common sense of th. Council. They were repeatedly wasting their time over these unnec-s.sary dicussions. Everyone knew what these buildings were intended f• •r, and to endeav- our to convert them into municipal offices for utterly different purposes was altogether beside the mark. Councillor Tinnion RAS a party to all that had been done. - .</p>
        <p>Councillor TINNION : No, sir. I have opposed it all through Alderman FAuteLotrent pointed out in regard to the aubstitu ion of wood block oring for asphalt paving that the asphalt bed been found in other p'acas to ha injurious to the mon's health. The MAYOU said they had estrong recommendation from the Inspector who inspected the police foto, that this alteration should be made for the health and com- fort of the men, and at the committee meting that had a strong bearing upon the discussion. Alderman HUTCHINSON Said he understood the differ- ence in 'the coat would be V 2. The MAYON aid it was about that.</p>
        <p>Lldprmtn MONKS said the police premises were not suitab'e. and could not be wade auitablr, for what Councillor 'Donlon bad suggested. He should have drawn up a report upon the matter. and not asked them to do se.</p>
        <p>The MAvon said it would not be wise to alter the buildings as suggented. With regard to the indication boards, they were necessary, and could be altered with- out very large expense if it were ever found necessary. At present it was impossible to allocate any of the municipal offices to the position suggested. If in course of time it wee requisite the Corporation could take any step they thought fir. Councillor Timms said be had not moved the amend- ment in any carpi g spitit. Hie only object, waste obtain an official intimation as Co whether what he suggested was possible or not. Sufis an intimation would satisfy him. The DRPIITY MAYOR, for the purpose of information, informed Councillor Tinnion that he had a copy of the Instructions to the architects containing the nizo of every room and the purposes for which they were to be used. That he believed was supplied to every member of the Counct, or was open to their Inspection, before the plans were adopted. Coon° Ilsr MINION : If you satisfy the public it I will do for me.</p>
        <p>_ Councillor 'WILLIAM ROBERTS said the indication board 4 would answer the question as to whether there were any rooms in the buildings act required for police purposes. The amendment was then put, and was lost, only three voting frr it. Councillor JANNI EVANS moved that the minute re- ferred to by Couacillar Smethurst be referred back, and complained of the numerous iltiratioue to the building. They had no information as .to the difference in the cost of the wood block and asphalting, and it was for the sak- of getting that information that he moved the amendment. The MAYOR said he was told the difference would be about £B2 Councillor TINSION : The asphalt pavement is not put!ltme</p>
        <p>- The 'MAYOR said it was not. I Alderman HUTOSINSO* observel that the wood block flooring would b.. much more convenient and durable ban asphalt moing. Councillor SataTßußsT seconded, and objected to a remark he said the Deputy Ddyter had made about members getting up and making fools of themselves. (Laughter). The DIPIITY MaTort I am not aware. that ,I said members got up to make fools of themselves. (Laugh- ter.) tit/Councillor SernTrionsT tieing foolish language then. (Laughter.) Councillor BURTON said a gentleman at one time the senior member ot the Cnuacii,ones said that a wise man at times changed his mind,but a fool never did(Lairghter.) If the Committee saw womething that was better, more serviceable and more converden, than what had teen arranged for originally they were wise in carrying it out.</p>
        <p>The amendment waa then put and defeated by a large majority. ine minutes were twin c,rried in thlvir enti:ety. TUE COUNTY SE TYLE)JEN T.</p>
        <p>Alderman FAIRCLOOGEI ra )vod the adoption of the mittutte of r h.• Finance Committee acid reterred to their ff,r of £24,000 with interest at 3 per cent, from the date on which Warrington became a County Borough as settlement of all claims by the County Council. The jlint inquiry had been he maid, a Very long one and cricy bad u,lle to a lot of trouble to arrive at a proposal which he hoped the County County would accept.</p>
        <p>Councillor A J JOLLEY seconded and the rainutep were COUNCILLOR JA \IE3 EVANS AND THE STANDING</p>
        <p>ORDERS. ARE HORSES MATERIALS?</p>
        <p>Couocillor Jofts Evews proposed the adoption cf the minutes of the Banit,iry Works C nnrnittee, which in- c'uded the purchss. of two hor-es from Mr D,vies of Wsrburtoo for £l4O —C-uncillor BAIRD seconded.</p>
        <p>Councillor JAMB 3 Evaxs eoquir,d wh t-isr toe stand- ing orders had been ccmpliid with in the purchase of these hwaes. The standing or&amp;rs i 'glued that all materials or labpur required 0 the extent of £2O or more Inu,t he advertised and c.)ntrac,,ed for. Did toe purchase of Untie horses come under that standing ord-r ?</p>
        <p>The Tows ELNRIC:I ,ACiViSe that tha standing order does not apply on he purchase of horseh It applies to the purchase of goods. Councillor EVANS : I take it that these horses ire rod ? (Laugriter ) If the order does no: apply to all sp,nding of monies I shall bring a resolution to alter it.</p>
        <p>Councillor TINNION HOW would ths Town Clerk rule something ot this sort P Supposs we made a con- tract with Borne profesiional gsatlemea t analyke qußutities for the Corporation.</p>
        <p>The TOWN CLERK Ui sf,rvices %plow come on,pr the head of labour. (Lauzhte•.) I fail to see how you could ask for tenders for horses.</p>
        <p>Alderman SurrON : It is an utter absurdity to apply the er ler to the purchase of hotels. Does Council:or Evans expect that if we advertised for horses somebody would send one on approval? (Laughter.) Councillor EVANS Thero are Corporations who ic- ' vito tenders for horses.</p>
        <p>Councillor jOHN ETANS said the custom was to appoint a sub-c ammitt eas ,a the pr, s qat case. The ruling nf the Town Clerk was taken and the work of the sub--orn- mit e, had be. n exceedingly well done. Ole of toe hoe s took the first rriz in the parade on Saturday. (Applause.) No UNIFORM HOLIDAY.</p>
        <p>Councillor SIIILLCOCY proposed the adoption of the minutes of the ocho I Attenclasce Committee and said that with r,,g,trd to the su:eestecl uniform holiday fr July 11th to August 9 h,very few of the school managers were inclined to adopt it, and things svou:d pror.ably be the same as last year.</p>
