        <p>A meeting of the G 44 C,rnnitee V73:4 held on t h.lrh April whoa it was resolved that Air iv R Wilson, 01 C lne, ha atm-lint() I the assrant gas engineer at a Ealary of £l5O ,3er annum. Al lerman FAIRCLOUGH moved the confirmation of the minutes, and. iu d.)ici.f, so, said that, notwi,Matanling the competition they had bad with the electric light_ (laughte)—the supply of f_,V4 fr..» the LDIIHIrd W.lrk4 during last veer had been 20.000 000 cuhio feet m ,re tnan it 17119 during the p, vi ycar, (clear, hear), The profi's for tile las', 12 mon'h —h. got the figures that day from 'dr Taylor—ere £2,737 5t 101. (Hear, hear, and. applause).</p>
        <p>Coo .cilhr TINNIOV obtorved that he was glad tlyn proats Isad been s Largo elniiderinc the hign price of coal and cannel and such litre things. The minutes were then %creed to. GYMNASIUN CO NITTEE.</p>
        <p>THE EDUCATION DEPARTNIEN AND PUYSICAL INSTRUCTION :FOR SCHOOL CUILDREN.</p>
        <p>It was repored at a rneetng of the Gymnasium Com- mittee that the total atteni vac() at th c!aqseo during the month of April wag 6,322 CuncPhr Trerwmer suornitted th confirmation of the minutes of the C)mmittee, and asked to be allowed to mnntion a matter nor ailu led to io the neintroe, ususeir, that the E lucatiln Department were now touch morr. particular in reference to the physical instillation that was to he given to the school children than they used to be. Thar ha I intim atei tiAt instet lof the usual time of one hour for instruc,loa it cam! I be nocesnry for the classes to have two half-hour.' iostructio I per week in- ste id. There was a ROW time tabl to h. rntip,anj th.. committ e would end av)ur to make it fit in to suit tbe COrl ^ninOO. OF the slhol!nil,,ro,s Councillor lIIILSK seconded, and the motion was agreed to. THE CORONATION CELEBRATIONS.</p>
        <p>The General Purposes Committee recommended tliat a loyal and corwratulatory address he presented to His Majesty the KM! on the occasion of hia nor mation. Toe committee considered the Government Eluoation Bill. It was resolved, that the further consideration be ad- j-iurned f-r one month • The committee also resolved that tend ,re be invited for the sae or !erring of the Feathers Hate]. and that the Mayor and the Coairman of the Gas, Elrctricity, ard Town Hall, Pirkg, and [laths Committees re appointed to carry out srronge- meets for illuminating and decorating the public build- ings on the occasion of the coronation, 'rho MAYOR, in moving the confirmation of the min- utes, sail subscriptions to the local coronation fund were not coming in goite as rapidly as they could wish Nold übt as it came n-arer the time, their loyalty would warm up and more mooey would come in. (Hear, hear) Alderman SUTTON secoad-d the confirmation of the minutes.</p>
        <p>Clunclllor SMETHURST askei whethor it Wil9 proposed to dec,rato all tse public bn•llings ? The M&amp;YDR : That mattrr has ben taken into ron- Bider ,tior. Ir all serms a matter of how much the Corn- mit'pe thinks it ii,•sir•blet oend.</p>
        <p>Councillor Witxtau Romenvs pointed out that if some definite announcement could be made about the gram to ths Sunday schools, it would very materially assist the Mayor's Coronation fund. There was a good deal of uncertainty whether the various schools were going to make their own appeal to tradesmen for subscriptions. If they were not going to do fon there would be an increase in the amount ssnt to the Mayor'e fund. Councillor JAMES EVANS said the Sunday school with which be wee connected had decided to eommunicate with the tradesmen who usually helped them inform- ing them that they would not be called upon for a sub- scription this year owing to the tract from the Mayor's fund. He thought if ether schools did the same, the subscriptions to the Mayor's fund weuld largely in- crew. Any deficit in the school funds would be made up by the officers and friends. The DEPUTY Maims stated•that at the meeting called to consider the subject it was found impossible to eat concerted action in regard to the schools subscriptions. lIISVPTSJ of the gentlemen present representing different schools, said the grant would not be sufficient to enable them to meet their expecsea. The meeting thought it could not go further than leave it to the authorities to do what they thought hest in the interests of the various schcols.</p>
        <p>Alderman HErratussott thoueht if it was understood by tradesmen that they would ivt be called upnn for school subscriptions there would be an increase of sub- scriptinns to the Mayor's fund. The MAYOR said he underatnod Vie intention was to leave the schools at liberty to do what they thought best. (Hear, hear.) The minutes were then passed,. •</p>
        <p>Primitive Methodist Synod.—The New Warrington Minister. _</p>
        <p>On Tuesday the Liverpool District Synod of Primitive Methodists at Blackburn was concluded. A resolution protesting against the Educational Bill was passed.—A resolution was passed express- ing satisfaction that one of the two ministers appointed as Connexional evangelists was the Rev W B Bird, of Southport, whose Christian character, gracious spirit, and success marked him out as specially adapted for the work.—The Governoient Temperance Bill was warmly welcomed as likely to diminish the spread of intemperance. The stations of local ministers for the ensuing year were as follows .—Preston Brook and Runcorn, W A Eyre; St. Helens, J (Allender Warrington J W Reed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>NOTES ON UA4VIES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON UA4VIES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>If the Warrington team continue to display such form as that which they exhibited on Saturday, a successful season can reasonably be anticipated. To accomplish the feat of gaining a complete victory over their Bolton rivals is certainly a feather in the cap i of the Warring- tonians, especially when it is borne n mind that the Bolton contingent have since the opening of the season amply demonstrated that they are a team not to be lightly reekoned with. Then again,it is many years since Warrington succeeded in defeating the Bolton eleven so that Saturday's victory is all the more acceptable. The outstanding feature of the match was the brilliant batting performance by J W Bibby, the Warrington captain. Rarely has Mr Bibby been seen to better advantage for, after an innings which embraced not a single family stroke, he carried out his bat with the capital total of 41 standing to his credit. He came in at a critical period of the match, but exhibited such a sound defence, c ,mbined with a capital attack at the onset, that the issue was always safe, provided someone stayed long enough with him. When he had got the measure of the bowling, his placing, especially on the offside, was executed with excellent judgment.</p>
        <p>The visitors won the toss, and batted first, on a wicket which was in favour of the bowlers. The two Bolton professionals, Ellis and Yost, were opposed by Lynes and J W Bibby. The Warrington captain met with success in his first over, for Vost,in trying to place one square, mistimed it, and was palpably leg before with only two runs on the board</p>
        <p>Howarth car&amp; next, but played a cautious game, the majority of the runs coming only at rare intervals, owing to the close fielding, from th 6 bat of Ellis. With the total at eight, a fine catch at the wicket by Hamp- son standing back to Lynes, was responsible for Ellis s dismissal, whilst four runs later Shepherd returned one to the professionaL</p>
        <p>W A Beswick now went to the wicket, and assisted Howarth to take the total to 22, when he failed alto- gether to stop an extra fast one from Lynes. Yates joined Howarth, who was exhibiting a sound defence. The score rose steadily, and Yates, profiting by a mis- take, hit out in resolute fashion.</p>
        <p>At 44 W H Podmore took the ball from his captain. The new bowler soon met with success, for Yates jumped out to a slow one, missed, and fell a victim to Hampson's dexterity behind the wicket. Feather came next, and was bowled first ball. Thewlis, after klacing Podmore to leg for four, was also tempted to hit, and Hampson was credited with his third wicket, the tele- graph board reading 50-7 4. Townson assisted Howarth to take the total to 69, when he had his off stump up- rooted. At 75 Howarth's patient innings came to an end. Podmore accepting a tame return, and Foster being bowled first ball, the innings closed for 75.</p>
        <p>The bowling of Lynes and Podmore proved very effective. The proTessional's record was 4 wickets for 28 runs, whilst Podmore was responsible for 5 wickets at a cost of 19 runs.</p>
        <p>Warrington commenced their innings with B Silcock and E A Connor, and the last named soon got to work, scoring nine in as many minutes and was then taken at extra cover. Disasters befel the locals in quick succes- sion, for without any addition to the total Ellis bowled J Callon and Gibbon.</p>
        <p>Lynes joined Silcock, who was exhibiting praise- worthy patience, the bowling being of a high order. The professional soon got to work, and after getting Ellis away to the leg bounda y, he cut the next ball for a like number. Ellis, however, had his revenge for Lynes touched one into the hands of short slip, Vost bringing off a brilliant catch. Four wickets were thus down for 19, so that the chances favoured Bolton. 4</p>
        <p>J W Bibby came next, and the Warrington captain proved to be in excellent form. Paying particular attention to Vost, he drove him for three, whilst in the next over Silcock also placed him for three. Runs came steadily, and a loud cheer greeted Bibby when he put Ellis to the leg boundary. At 44 Silcock returned one to Ellis, and the amateur retired for an invaluable 13 made with his vistomary patience. J Briggs came next and quickly added ten, when he was bowled by Cunliffe who had superseded Yost.</p>
        <p>W H Podmore was bowled off his pads without scor- ing, so that the game again took a turn in 'Bolton's favour. Thompson joined Bibby, and Vost resumed at 60, in place of Cunliffe. Slowly, but surely, the Boffon aggregate was reached, and after Bibby had pladed Yost for four and three in one over, he turned his atten- tion to Ellis, and driving him for a couple made the winning stroke, a loud cheer greeting the victory. Mr Bibby continued his merry career, but at 91 lost the company of Thompson.</p>
        <p>Goodall vickly added six, and then stumps were drawn, Warrington's aggregate being 99 for eight wickets On returning to the pavilion, Mr Bibby was accorded an ovation for his brilliant performance.</p>
        <p>In view of the Bolton tears's visit to Warrington, the bowling performances of Ellis, the well-known..,pro- fessional, in other matches of the seasoa will prove of interest. In the first match against Sale he secured the 'whole of the ten wickets for 25 runs ; against Hey- wood he had six wickets-for 18 runs and at Manchester on Wednesday he was credited with six more wickets, bringing his total for the three matches to 22. At War- rington his average was 4 for 27. Ellis has had a long and honourable connection with Bolton ; but at least two Central Lancashire clubs tried their utmost to get him to terminate that connection.</p>
        <p>The negotiations which were begun last autumn between Mr /I N Turner, of the St Helens Cricket Club, and Mr C A Alcock,, who had charge of the arrange- ments for the Australian visit, have been brought to a successful issue. There were some questions of detail which were left in abeyance until Major Wardill and the Australian team had arrived in England, and at the earliest possible moment Major Wardill communicated with Mr Turner, confirming the promised visit to St Helens. The dates 'fixed are Monday, Tuesday, and Wednesday, September Ist, 2nd. and 3rd. St Helens is the only town club in England fortunate enough to secure a fixture with the Australians, every other request having been refused.</p>
        <p>An important step has since been taken in connection with the visit of the Australians to St Helens. The Mayor (Colonel W W Pilkington) has been approached and he has expressed his cordial approval of the vro- posal It is intended to make the occasion an import- ant function, and it wip be managed by a town's c om- mittee. Neither Lancashire nor Sussex are playing on the dates arranged for the match, while Yorkshire have only a friendly game on, and it is said that invitations to play may be given to W G Grace, Ranjitsinhji, A C MacLaren,C B Pry, A Ward, J T Tyldesley, S Barnes, W Rhodes, G fl Hirst, J Sharp, W S Cuttell, C Smith, and A A Lilley. The match is described in the official list as one between the Australians and an "All-England Eleven," so that it is sure to be more than a districti fixture.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>Cycling.</head>
        <p>Cycling.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening a workingman cyclist met with a rather nasty accident opposite the Norton Arms Hotel, Wilderspool i -road. He was turning from the Old- road into Wilderspool-road and momentarily losing con- trol of his machine ran into a cab, which was going in the direction of the town. The front wheel of his bicycle became locked in one of the cab wheels and the bicycle and its luckless rider were dragged along the road for some distance. Miraculously, it seemed, the man escaped serious injury, but the bicycle was, of course, completely wrecked. In justice to the cab driver, it should be stated that he was on his right side of the road and exercising every reasonable care.</p>
        <p>As a lady cyclist, Hiiss Laura Austen was riding on the road from Faversham to. Whitstable last May she fell into an unprotected trench, and has since suffered from spinal affection. The other day Miss Austen sued the Corporation of Faversham for damages sustained and was awarded £250.</p>
        <p>A. new record has been established for a cycle journey between Paris and Madrid by the French cyclist Muller, who traversed the distance in four days, five hours,and fifty minutes.</p>
        <p>A London evening paper states that a cyclist in Southern France recently overtook a peasant with a donkey-cart. The patient beast was making but little progress, BO the benevolent cyclist, putting his left hand against the back of the cart and guiding his machine with the other band, pushed so hard that the donkey, taking fresh courage, pulled his load success- fully up to the top. When the summit was reached the peasant burst into thanks to his benefactor. "It was good of you, indeed, monsieur! ' he protes ed ; "I should never in the world have got up the hill with only one donkey."</p>
        <p>The Bristol postmen were recently the selected vic- tims of a warped miscreant, who, during dinner hour, got access to the P.O. bicycle shed, and badly gashed the tyres of no fewer than twenty-two cycles—privately owned by the men—which were temporarily thrown out of use, greatly to the inconvenience of the letter- carriers, who had long rounds.</p>
        <p>For ucoul oF hours on WTdoeviriy the chinch bell at Pontefract rung to warn those whom it might concern to attend in the vestry and pay kb,' eallrevu's the tithe rent charge clue to (h 9 vicar. Most of the people,' how- ever, wait civil the coliec'er ea:la upon them for the money. The ammnt prod varies fp-ma a few coppire up to 41.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED BURGLARY AT SANKEY.</p>
        <p>Prisoners Before the Magistrates.</p>
        <p>At the Warrinston Occasional Court, on Saturday morning, before M.. F A Frost, Patrick Griffiths and John Murphy, strangers to the district, were charged with feloniously breaking and entering the Great Sankey Schools, and ste4ling therefrom a coat and an overcoat, the property of the Sch,)ol B ard. Sergeant Barris stated that he was in company with P.C. Houghton in Liverpool-road, at half-past one that morning, when they heard footsteps coming tram the direction of Warringron, and which seemed to stop at 1 the schools. A little later they heard the noise of some one c'imbing he school wall and thei a sin ishing of , glas.. •Witnsas and the constable tot over the wall and found that a window at the back of the schools had been broken. P.O. Houghton took up a position at the window- and witness aroused Mr Clarke, the school- master. They then went into the schoolroom and found the two defendants crouching down in a corner. Griffiths had a coat under his armond the overcoat was found near. In reply to the charge Griffiths said : "It Is false ; I never saw the overcoat," and Murphy laid : "I am innocent." The officer asked for a Temand for the prisoners until Friday and it was granted.</p>
