      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0086" n="art0086">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Warrington Union School Attendance Committee.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the School attendance Committee of the Warrinuton Union was held in the Board Boom at the Workhouse on Weduesiay. There were present: Rev Father Wilson, Dr Murray, Messrs T Elalton, Brookes, Houghton, Mather, Forshaw and the clerk (1r J C Sutton.) It was resolved to re elect Mr Halton as Chairman of the committee, and Mr Brookes, Vice-chairman. St. Luke's Church.—Sale of Work.</p>
        <p>A sale of work in connection with St. Luke's Church was held in the Conservative Club Assembly Room on Wednesday. The opening ceremony was performed by Mrs John White in the presence of a good attendance, the Vicar (tiev E CE Carleton) presidinz. The objects of the sale were briefly ex- plained by the curate-in-charge (Rev 11. It Wilde) who pointed out that that was the first sale of work which had been held in connection with St Luke's. It had been found necessary because the collections barely covered the church expenses. Navy surplices were urgently needed, and the Prayer books and hymn books were in a dilapidated condition. The harmonium was far from satisfactory and they would have to con- sider the necessity of getting a new one unless some kind friend made them a present of an organ. The churchyard also reguiled, improvement. The rev gentleman mentioned the fact that part of the endowment of the church which was invested in Great Central Railway Stock was at present yield- ing no interest whatever, the loss being about £25 per ,year. It the sale proved successful the wardens might see their way to give part of the proceeds to Mr John White, the treasurer of he Curacy Fund, to help to cover some of this loss. It was also desirable that the wardens should have some reserve fund to fall back upon in case of necessity. —ln calling upon Mrs White to open the sale, tke Vicar said that both Mr and Mrs White were warm friends to the parish.—Mrs White then opened the Sale and sain that St Luke's was a very poor parish and the time bad arrive I when much of the good work which Mr Wilde was engaged in required pecuniary assistance. She hoped all present would do their best •to make the sale a success. A hearty vote of thanks was accbrded to Mrs White, on the proposition of Mr Jackson seconded by Mr Gleave, and the business of the day commenced.—The stalls had been very attractively arranged, and the • room was brightly decorated with bunting. Vie following is a list of the stall holders :—Congregational stall, Mesdames Jackson, Taylor, Sutton, Williams, Oldfield, Ellison, Earthen], Jenks, Armstrong, Appleby and Gleave and Miss Leach ; Sewing Guild stall, Misses Mills, Wheeldon, Jackson and Taker; Holy Trinity stall, Mesdames Carleton. Randall. Forshaw, Welsby and Ferguson,: ice cream stall, Mrs Peers ; Young Men stall, • Messrs Barr, Ackenhead, Clete, Ashcroft, Horrobin and VA Jones; doll stall. Misses Jackson and Bennie The proceeds of the sale amounted to £67 3s which is regarded as very satisfactory.</p>
