      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Halton-road, Runcorn.—Annual</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES. Halton-road, Runcorn.—Annual</p>
        <p>Sermons.</p>
        <p>The annual Sunday School sermons were preached on Sunday at morning, noon, and evening servos The pulpit morning and evening was occupied by the 11,v W George, a former minister in the Runcorn circuit. who gave two impressive discourses on the importance, place, and character of Sah'ath school wor The scholars, wha had been most carefully traieei by Mr J Brown acquitted themselves right nobly in all the musical selections and fully maintained the past traditions of the Sunday School. At the morning service the principal pieces were : " Raise the Standard," " Be glad and rejoice." and "The Battle ef Life." The last-named bad a delightfully martial tone about it,chiefly in the chorus,which greatly comm nded it to the large congregation present!. ma afternoon was devoted to a service of praise, at which Mr F J Norman presided. An intereetiee and instruc- tive address was given to te scholars by Mr T Dodd, of . Widnes. The special hymn. were : "The OEOI flowers," " Happy little children " (sung by scholars under 12), "There is a bright and happy home," and " Hail to the King." The second piece by the infants was repeated by special request at the evening service, The principal piece in the evening was " Come unto Me," the solo part being beautifully rendered by a scholar 13 years of age,whib the chorus received mimic. able treatment from the children and chnir. An appro- priate closing piece was the " Children's evening hymn." The church was crowded to overflowing quiet an hour before the commencement of the evening ser- vice, and many were unable to gain admission. The organ was presided over by Mr R Johnson, Too offer• tories hunted to £3B.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>Buckle Street RS.A.—The Sunday</head>
        <p>Buckle Street RS.A.—The Sunday</p>
        <p>_ Tram Service.</p>
        <p>There was a large ait:rodanee et an oven meriting of the above society which was held on Sui,day. Mr f S Steel occupied the chair, and Mr B Farrar (secretary of Wycliffe P.S.A ) was the speaker. The Warrington Male Voice Choir, under the conductorstiip of falfr S H•ssall, efficiently r, wiered " Lead kindly Li,nr,to The Crusaders," and " Perrect in Christ." The Chair- man said he thought Sunday carnhling and piar,d2 S. tug were the h remant things which filled the mind r of many in this town, and thee, w-te matt lei they society shou it setk to rectify, So that they might regard the Sabbatla in a right and proper miuner. The enormoui ;railing on Sundays in this town was very deplor ,ble. It w s not so much the large shopkeep rs who were reSponsible for this state of th:ngs as the small shopker pee.. Wr at influence, asked the Chairman, would the Sunday school Ibo able to eieit upen the children who spent their coppers at these shops on the Sabbath day ? Parents should rel use to, give their children money on Sundays, and then thee° shops weuld soon have to close. Another matter he wished to speak abbot was the conduct of many youths in S,nkey- treet who did their utmost to cause aneoyance to pedestrians. He was pleased to know that the pollee w,re takrng FtepF to stop I:he nuisance. Further, he wished to ep 'air about Sun- day trame. The Sabbath day WAS IleeP.Sary is a thy of rest, and yet the. Corporation no: r Ws, worked the carmen long hours during the week, fur they compelled them to work on Sundays. Other towns managed wi'h- mr; Sunday trams, and he thought Warringt n might also do without them. Mr Farri,r acquierned in the le- Marks ol the Chairman, arid laid great stress op-1n the ' al,rming increase of labour en Sundays, declaring that it was appalling to 'funk our S•btaths were becoming more and more like the Coetinental Sunda .The sp aker said the P.S. A. movem nt was now representative of 60.000 men, all of wh-rn wetei determined to do their utmost to rescue th, 60,000 who annually fell victims to drink. The four essentials he thought they as P.S.A. hr i Voter) might advantageously adopt were " S TOCIG in virile, wise in their efforts, tender iii their hearts, and strong in faith." He left with them the greetings of Wycliffe P S A.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>Presentation to the Rev. Father Crowe, 0.5.8.</head>
        <p>Presentation to the Rev. Father Crowe, 0.5.8.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening the members of the Warrington Catholic Association assembled at the Aerneiation ram, in order to make la presentation to the Rev Father Crowe, OS 8., now curate of S. Peter's Church, Sr al- street,Liverpool. Father Crowe, who was until recently scomoected with St Alban', t hurcn, Warrington, is a late president of the association, in which ho took a great amount of Interest. The members felt his removal from the town very keenly, and it was decided to make him ,1 a presentation. A committee, conaistinr of M este r Mather (chairman), 0 1..01e (treasurer), C Mrwron (secretary), A H Cummings, T Gibighan, T Reardon, M Cunningham, A Miller, and J Riley, was formed to carry this out, and the eff,rt m t with the greatest euccese on all sides. Father Crowe was apuroached to lee what kind of present;would be,the most suitable,and selected a chalice. The chalice,which w•s made to iNtber Crows'. own design,is of solid silver;studded with amethysts,and eagravrel with the.emblerns of the Benedictine order. The inscription is as follows :—" P,esented to tbe Rev Fatter Crowe by the members of the Cathelic Asarciation, Warrington May 5 Ei, 1902 " It is the work of Messrs Hayes and Finch, Li verpool.—The meet- ing was presided over by the Rev Father Whittle, who was supported by the Rev Fathers Crowe, Wilson, and Gibbons, and Messrs Mather, Boyle, J Eckersiev, Vernon, Newton, Owen Tole, and M Cutinitagharn.—ln calling upon Mr Mather to make the presentation, the Rev Father Whittle said hietbought he would be expressing the opinion of all present when he said they were de- lighted to have their old friend Father Crowe amongst them. He knew it was to the regret of every member of St Alban'e Mission that Father Crowe was called from their midst to work in Liverpool. be bad the esteem and he might add, the affi clion o every member of the mission. (Hear, hear.) He re-f ' ferred to Sr. Alban's as the mother Catholic Church of Warrington and spoke of the now numerous branches which had spread out from the old tree. The new dis- tricts had taken large numbers from their congregation, but he gloried in he old church yet. Bo wished, in his own node, to thank Father Crowe for his loyal ser- vice duel, g hie four years' work with biM. He had always done his work thoroughly and efficiently in the parish,and the people had appreciated it. (Applause.)— Me Mather said Father Crowe hd been a pri-ft who had commanded the respect and love of all. He had pleasure in presenting the ehalice to Father Crowe on behalf of the committee and members, and hoped he would be long spared to lige it for the benefit of his OW/1 soul and the souls of those under his charze.(Applanee.)— Father Crowe, in responding. thanked Father whittle and fdr Mather for the kind tenns in which they had epoken .of him. The large number of people gathered together that evening was an evidence of the cordial good will and ream% they felt for the clergy wile minis- tered over them. Be thanked them for allowing him to I select the present, an he felt sure that chose who had beau Privileged to see it aud to have the design ex- plaine 1 to theca, while the chalice was in its unfinished state, would agree with him that when completed it would be a real work of art and a treasure. He took the presentation as an expression of thanks for the work</p>
        <p>=a</p>
        <p>which he had done amongst them, and he was incised gratclul. In concluaior, he assured them thiat when the chalice was completed, and had been consecrated, be would give the first Masa for the benefit of tLte associa- . . . .</p>
        <p>tion.(App'aus, ).—Mr Boylo alao sp )ke and alluded W thR gcd work which Fathrr Crowe bad done for the</p>
        <p>aseCoiatiop.</p>
        <p>A CRUEL FRAUD AT WARRINGTON.—CHEATING A to.</p>
        <p>SERVANT. A very singular case came before Mr F Mori cs and otl . . _ _</p>
        <p>magistrates at the Warrington Pol.ce Court, on Monday. when John Kilroy, Cropper e brow, was charged with obtain. frig 23s by means of a trick from Elizabeth Fryer, on April 30th. He Oeaded guitty.—The Chief Constable add tuisV when the Bench had heard the facts of the case they would ' agree with him that this was a cruel and heartless piece or robbery and deception. The facts were peculiar and unusual. The complainant, llztbeth Fryer, was a domestic servant. and up to a few dais ago was In the employ of a geutleman in Bold street Although 30 years of age she was of a veil simple not to say silly, disposition, in fact, she was one of the most confiding females he had ever met with. She appeared to have gone out on Sunday, the 20th of April to have her "night out," having told her employer she was going to visit bee mother at Moore whom she helped to support. She met the prisoner and they became acquainted. Faint iarity seemed to have ripened with lightniug-Ike rapidity beeause it ter- minated in her spending the nlht at a lodging house with the prisoner She was rather short of intellect, and one et that class of pet eons whicit seemed to be a particular prey men like the prisoner. an.t he thought entithse to the fa protection of the law. She went back to her situation the nest day with the plausible story that she had been with her mother all night. Eventually prisoner saw her on the 30tb of April lad tol l her he wanted to get married at once bet was short of money, and asked her to give him some for the sure. eie of buying the wedding ring. Dazzled by the prof' pact of being married she gave him a sovereign. He. toOlt</p>
        <p>IP</p>
        <p>_1</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>rneasur-ement of her finger and said he would</head>
        <p>the rneasur-ement of her finger and said he would</p>
        <p>, andalbuy the ring at Etiscance's. He did not do Gale , The woman again absented herself from her Atuation, stayed with him at the INlging house, thinking she nee going t be married almost immediately. Prisoner sabse' quently came again and asked her for another 10s cons— p ainanthad n more money,or she would probably haveglven it to him. He told her his name was Charles Williamson an that, he worked at Greening's wire works, and held responsible position, had a good wage, and also that he 11'1704 in Grosfield-street. His name, however, was not Cherie Williamson, but John Kilroy, and he did not occupy • responsible position at Greening's wire works or anywhere else. The only position he occupied was at street corner*: and he was a runner for bookmakers. The woman had parted with her money thinking she was going o be married. Information %V is given to the police, and t , Superintendent Mon-,ghati saw the prisoner on Saturdar He said to Kilroy " I hear you hate got married." and the prisoner replied "No, lam not married. I have two children to keep now." As a matter of fact he had. He was then told of the complaint that had been made against him by the woman, and he said " Yes, I did; and I spent this money 11 borate. lam net going to marry h'-r or anybody." lie flee' simply deceived the woman by a tissue of falsehoods•—Super. intendent Monaghan said he knew the man as a runner for, bookmakers He worked a little occasionally.—The CHO' Genet nble said this constituted prisoner's 11th appearance. and he had been previously convicted of theft and gambling. —lie was sentto gaol for three months with hard Isbour.</p>
        <p>A GUIDE TO "Tag Istiono.m_No place is easier to reach from almost any part of the kingdom than tha. sea Isle of Ma popular a pleasureo, for it has become so health resort that every facility is placed at the disposal at Intending visitors, and the way .thither smoothed Sr preciably. One means by which difficulties in the WO of reaching the Isle of Mau are readily solved is the offi ci l uid e, which is published by the official Board of be Advertising, and which maye had post free on applie tion either at Douglas or at 27, Imperial-buildhap Ludgate Circus, London:, This guide is a veritable Mlo_,° of information on all points relative to the island. 12. w natural beauties are well krown to tens of thousiWi; who have seen and appreciated them, but there are stilt some, strange as it nay seem, who have never been tf, hof Man, and for them the book is especial the Lie le, as it will draw their attention to a spat . is remarkably beautiful and considering the extent u" the island, very diversified. The climatic conditione are" generally sneaking, of the best, and eittur la.Douklas °; the outskiits will be found entertainment or recrestla.r to suit every taste. The guide is profusely illustratel a capital map of the island showiug the roads (g°(!. roads for cycling shown in red) and points of inteiest!'i various parrs of the island, while it also contains a 111/ of the hotelr, b)arding and lodging arid familial°, accomodation, houses (furnishe(l and unfurnished) t° let, car and other fares, &amp;e.</p>
