        <p>W. ROBERTS, 36, Park-street, Rhosildue, Wrexham, writes, Nov. 4th : " I write to thank you for the effect VENO'S SEAWEED TONIC has had upon me. I suffered in 1892 from Influeort, which left me with nervous debility, constipation, dizziness and headache ; but now lam thankful.to say I feel stronger ; my appetite is much better and my food digests properly : indeed, every dose seems to MBAS me stronger. Your SEAWEED TONIC does more than you claim for it ; I shill strongly racommend it to all my friends.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>ASTHAI ANp BRONCEIITIS CURED.</head>
        <p>ASTHAI ANp BRONCEIITIS CURED.</p>
        <p>Ml 3 Martin Bill, The Hen, Shifnal, Salop, writes :—"I have bad what the doctor called bronehial asthma, and be said I hould never be cured, but thanks be to God and VENO'S LICEIVSING Cobalt CU= I am better nowithan I have been for six years, and since I have been taking your Cough Cure! can lie down and have a good night's rest. It seems a tiodsend to me. I have recommended your Cough Cure to several of my friends, and shall keep ors doing so. lam sure it will be a great blessing to soft rers, I tried everything that people told me,but nothing eNd me a bit of good. Dating the winters I had to sit in a chair three months at 1 time because of the shortness of breath and a choking sensation, but all the,c has gone and I am now looking very well.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>VEIVO'S SEIVVEED TONIC</head>
        <p>VEIVO'S SEIVVEED TONIC</p>
        <p>is prepared from natural Seaweed, combined 4,lth table extracts, forming a high-class. P;armaceutical preparation of the highest medicical ar.civity. It cures deep-seated complaints of the stom aC;o, liver, kidneys and blood, possessing all the healing, strengthening and curative power possible. It Is especially good for " habitual constipation." Price 1.. 111. and 23. 11.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE</head>
        <p>VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE</p>
        <p>is a scientific remedy composed of ingredients never before used in Great Britain. for the speedy cure of all chest, throat and lung affections. It cuts the phlegm, brings it away easily, sbps a cough naturally, strengthens the lungs and gives perfect ease in breath- ing. The first few dase3 will convince you of its vast superiority over ordinary cough mixtures or any of the emulsions. Price ls. 11d. and 2i. 91 per bottle. Sold by chemists and medicine vendors everywhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>Kettero to tbe Ebitor.</head>
        <p>Kettero to tbe Ebitor.</p>
        <p>Sunday Trams and 'Buses. Sir,—At a meeting of the vestey of Emmanuel Church (Reformed Church of Eogland) on Tuesday last a reso- lution was unanimously passed protesting against the continuous running of the electric cars on Sundays. It is considered that seeing the running of the new cars forms a Corporation business it concerns the whole community and is different to the running of convey- ances belonging to private firms or individuals. It how- ever, there is some reason making it imperative for the cars t) run on Sundays they surely need not run oftener than the buses cid which they may be said to super- sede, and they ought not to run at all during the usual hours of Divine &amp;rvice. .</p>
        <p>I have to ask you to give this publicity through the medium of your paper as a quicker method than by memorializing the Corporation. Thanking you in antici- pation. I am yours faithfully, HENRY GANDY, Secretary to Vestry.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>May 13t 1902.</head>
        <p>May 13t 1902.</p>
        <p>Sir,—By your courtesy I have on many occasions given expression through your columns to my viewe on matters of public interest amongst them being the constantly recurring question of the observance of the Sunday or Sabbath or Day of rest or Lord's Day as it is variously called. Having previously taken so prominent ' a part in correspondence upon this subject, I refrained last week fromo raising an objection to the running of the new electric tram cars on Sunday, and am glad to see that another correspondent, Me Andrew Scriven, is , alive to the importance of the question. Let me first of all offer a sincere expression of gratifica- tion to the Tramways Committee for the extreme care welch they have evidently taken, by means of guard wires' Isc, to protect the public from physical danger, and also for the exceedingly fine cars which they have provided. When the tramway serice is complete it will undoubtedly prove of immense service to the town. But while this is too why should the Tramway Committee and the Town Council spoil, in the opinion of many, this good and useful means of municipal passenger traffic by running a full service of cars on the Day of Rest ? Where , is the necessity for this Sunday service ? Are not the members of the Town Council the Guardians of the civic ' Sabbath as distinguisned from the Religious Sabbath. No man or body of men in thia country may compel another man to attend • place of worship; but on the other band, what right has the Town Council, that is the community to employ its servants in unnecessary work on the Sunday ? Whatever may be said in favour of a strictly limited service of cars on the Day of Rest for possibly necessa.ry purposes it cannot be denied that probably three-fourths of the traffic on Sunday last was simply and solely for the purposes of pleasure, to provide which Same bad to be employed on the cars and, I suppose, at the power house on the Day of Rest, It is a well-known physio- logical fact that " a seventh day of rest, instituted by God, is essential to the bodily health and mental vigour of man in every station of life." This being so, instead , of setting the bad example of running the cars on Sunday the Town Council should set the good example of pro- tecting the whole community from all unnecessary encroachments on the Day of Rest. The words of Burke, " A nation that neglects the Sabbath soon sinks into barbarism and ruin ; civilised man cannot bear the -pressure of seven days' work and worry in the week," might well be remembered by our local legislators. That the wcrking men of lbs working class town should tamely submit to have their Day of Rest thus filched away from them bit by bit is truly astonishing. They might remember the words of that great political economist, John Stuart Mill, who said "Operatives are perfectly right in think-! lug that if all worked on Sunda?, seven days' work would have to be given for six days' wages." If, as I am infiirmid, the towns of Aberdeen, Batley, Chester- field, Cambridge, Cardiff, Dover, Dewsbury, Dundee, Greenock, Hyde. Huldersfield, Ipswich, Llncoln, Leith, Newport (Mon) Northampton, Paisley, Perth, Plymouth and Wrexham can do without Sunday tramcars, why , cannot Warrington also do without them ? There are still a number cf people who hold with I intense conviction the belief that the weekly day of rest I is not only an institution fraught with great blessings to the physical and mental nature of man but also that is is a divine gift. Pas* history has proved that the neglect ' or imprrper tpe ot this day has brought calamty in its train to nations and individuals. It will not be different with Warriogton if as a town it persists in setting at de- fiance laws written by God in human nature as well as in the pages of Holy Writ. Let no one brush this letter aside by saying it comes from " a parson." The suLject has not in this letter been presented from its religious, but from its physical aide. Much more could be said upon the religious as- pect of the case, but I forbear because I am hopeful that such a protest will arise from right minded people in the town as will cause the Town Council SO to alter their arrangements as to s.cure for their servants and the community generally, the priceless boon of the Day Of Rest. Faithfully yours, EDWARD T REED.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Ihe Oliver Street Improvement.</head>
        <p>Ihe Oliver Street Improvement.</p>
        <p>Sir,— Would you kindly alcw me epace to draw the attention of tho,e in authority to the above. S3tne wo years ago there was a loud cry raised about urgent improvements being required in this neighbourhood tO relieve the heavy traffic by opening into Orford-lane, and the owners of the " Wheat Sheaf Hotel " promiscd to give the sum of four hundred at. d fifty pounds towards this very urgent matter, conditionally that their plans were passed. On the various statements made the Licensing committee sanctioned the came. True we have the improvement in a new hotel completed and now that is accomplished, the street improvement does not appear as urgent as we were led to believe, other- wise it would have been completed also. If the above sum has been paid over, what has be- come et it, for a continuance of tha present state of things is a gross injus:ice to the public of the neighbour- hood and the owners of the above hotel. Surely all the energies &amp;c., of the Council, are not beicg devoted to electric and coronation matters. while the arnet im- provement is shelved. Hoping to see a move made, or an explanation given. I remain, yours Etc.. REMINDER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Dictatorial Trams.</head>
        <p>Dictatorial Trams.</p>
        <p>Charged at Stockport this week, with obstructing the tram route in Beaton-lane. Stockport, Jamfs Egerton, a dray man, pleaded that he had only two alternitives, either to go on the wrong side of the road or de:ay the car—The magistrates, in dismissing tilt, charge, stated that the tramway autherities, even though they wire the Corporation, bad no more right of way than anyone else.</p>
