      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>WARRINGTON HORSE PARADE.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON HORSE PARADE.</p>
        <p>A Successful Event.</p>
        <p>In a manner characteristic of a progressive indus- trial centre, Warririgton celebrated on Saturday,a.,e incoming of May. Until last year Warrington ita for a considerable period possessed the distin ,tion of being one of the few large towns in this (1 riet , which held no organized May festival, but welve months ago, thanks to the commendable At _marts•of a number of well-known townsmen, a, .achaome was brought to a successful issue for the ,dukir, of an annual horse parade. Truly, there,it lirtle if any- thing, to mark the. " merrie Tont •eseifiesiiv in : a celebration of this descriptior butt parade like that which was witnessed on cs,,. saistartiay reflects to some. extent the spirit oft ,",t times, and perhaps the picturesque old-tim. 4,l,.festiyi,ie___ ""',,,"'aii are organized in rural clistrir.,o I,7oJiiin hardly be suitable day life in Warrington. amid the .turmoil e:fery Then again, a very laud able 'purpose is served by oarado, inasmuch as it encourages an annual horse the drivers to, tus_E _a ,a greater interest in the animals under they ewe. Saturday's procession was ample .. evidera that the horses had been as well cared for , as C" A be desired, and all who witnessed it were agre."l, that the parade reflected credit upon the (M. The success of the event was such as to fulfil Ake most sanguine sxpecttvions, and the Committee to whom this gratifying result was due, must have been fully compensated for their efforts to make the parade worthy of the town. The preparations necessarily entailed the easrifice of a great deal of time and trouble, especially on the part of the energetic lion secretaries, Messrs George Venn and T H Pretty, and to these gentlemen the warmest praise must be accorded A new departure had been made this year in the offering of urizss and certificates of merit to the drivers for clean and tidy appearance of horses, harness and vehicles,and diplomas—presented by the R.S.P.C.A.—to encour- age kind treatment of the animals under their charge. The money prizes ranged in amount from 3s 6,1 to £1 and it is worthy of note that very few I competitors failed to secure an award. The weather conditions, on which so much de- pended, were very unpromising during Saturday morning. The sky was overclouded, at intervals there were sharp showers,and a strong wind tended to increase the unpleasantness of being out of doors. No wonder then that there were anxious misgivings as to whether success would attend the afternoon function. At 11 o'clock the ju •ges com- menced the arduous task of adjudicating upon the turnouts entered for competition. The turnouts, numbering 120, assembled on the Fair Ground at the rear of the Blue Coat School. and a large number of townspeople were present to witness the judging. The general excellence of the horses and vehicles entered for competition rendered the awarding of the prizes a far from easy task, but the judges discharged their duties with the greatest care, and their decisions gave every satisfaction. After completing their work, the judges, who spoke very highly of the turnouts, were entertained at luncheon at the Lion Hotel, The interest taken by the public in the parade was demonstrated by the large numbers of spectators who lined the route shortly before the time announced for the start from the Fair Ground. Happily the elements were now in a much more favourable mood ; the clouds had rolled away and during the afternoon there was not a single shower to mar the pleasure of either spsctators or those concerned in the parade. With striking promptitude the pro- cession left the Fair Ground headed by the Borough Band, which discourse i lively selections. Titan came the Warrington Fire Brigade,with the engines " Major " and " Captain," gaily bedecked with arti- ficial flowers, Sze, for the occasion. Superintendent Turner was mounted on a whit,- horse, and, with shining helmets, the members of the brigade , presented a smart appearance which attracted general attention. The splendid turnouts of Messrs Peter Walker and Sons followed and in themselves undoubtedly constituted one of the most prominent features of the parade The horses were big, powerful creatures, and thei. admirable condition was abundant testimony to the attention which must be bestowed upon them. The animals were gaily caparisoned with flowers, bells, and other ornaments,whilst the vehicles were adorned with portraits of the King and Queen and numerous patriotic devices. Messrs Walker', Band, in their bright uniforms, followed the firm's turn- outs, and after them came the Wilderepool Fire Brigade with the engine" Empress." Superinten- dent Inca was in charge and the appearance of the</p>
        <p>brigade formed an agreeable break in the long line of turnoute. Horses and vehicles representing almost every important industry of the town now followed in quick succession. There were horses of almost every class, from heavy cart-horses to the smart light animals used by the various tradespeople, and all were denorated with a taste and originality which was very pleasing to the eye. On some of the vehicles the products of the firms were exhibited. Even the humble donkey found a place in the parade, Mr Alf Taylor, with his usual enterprise, having as his turnout a donkey hich bore a poster with the legend "This animal is noted for hard ware like Al? Taylor's won- derful boots." The last section of the parade was headed by St Paul's band. Altogether there were close on 300 turnouts, and this number is all the more satis'actory when it is reealle I that last year there were only 137. some of the principal firms taking part, in addition to the one already men- tioned, were Messrs Greenall,Whitlev and Company, Warrington Slate Company, Limited, Messrs J Buckley and Company, and Pearson and Knowles, Coal and Iron Company, Limited. Another commendable object which the Om- mitten had in view was that the parade might he the means of affording pecuniary assistance to two of our most worthy institutions—the Infirmary and the District Nursing Association. There was quite an army of collectors, under the direction of Mr Joseph Taylor, secretary of the Infirmary Work- ingmen's Committee, who exerted themselves to the utmost to obtain the contributiuns of the spec- tators. A number of nurses seated in a landau also lent assistance in this di:ection, and it was difficult to resist their persuasive powers. Interpersed among the various turnouts were vehicles which bad been specially arranged for the reception of contributions. It is gratifying to learn that the collection yielded the substantial sum of over £3O. The procession traversed the following ;rout e :—Win wick-road, Tanners-lane, Froghall-lane, Cresfield-street, Sankey-street, Buttermarket-street, Church-street, round lamp near the Parish Church, Church-street, Mersey-street, Bridge-street, Horse- market-street, Winvvick-street, and Pinners-brow to Orford-lane.—After remaining a short time on the Fair Ground the turnouts dispersed, and the horse parade of 1902 was a thing of the past.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>OFFICIALS.</head>
        <p>THE OFFICIALS.</p>
        <p>The following officials were in a great measure re- sponsible for the success of the event; —President, Sir G Greenatl, Bart. ; Vice-presidents, Messrs El P Densharn, James Dodds, William Fletcher, C Jackson 11 Ames, William Long, Colin Maclver, Henry M•liing, Alderman F W Moralts, James More, Percy M-irtimer, Colonel Ridgway, Alderman Roberts, Peter Rylands, James Tinsley, Frank Tinsley, James Tinsley, jun.. R 1 Wynne- Jonei ; Recerilion Committee, Alderman Roberts. and Messrs 13 P Denham, Willi 011 Fletcher (Whitles),James Almro, W Peter Rvlands ; Sub-committee. Messrs Broads", Burrows, Robsen Cborlev, E Iterwood, Lowe, El Milliug, jun.. Stephen Mss, JAMBS Newton, Muskett, J E Pickton, F R P,erpoint, II G3rdrn Rterts, Richard Spann, H Ferguson, in]. Ivan Fulton Smith. Hon. seer( taries, Messrs George Venn, and T Fl Pretty; fin. trea, urer, Mr J Fulton Smith ; Judges, Messrs John Abbott, Thomas Burton, T Charnock, G Fairhurst, J Farr- worth, James Gould, Joseph Knowles, J Lomas, John Longshaw, Gorge Marsh, H Ma wdesley, E Thomas Percival, N Filling, J Stuart and Robert Turner.</p>
      </div>
