      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>CORPORATION TRAMWAYS. INTERESTING STATEMENT BY ALDERMAN ROBERTS.</head>
        <p>THE CORPORATION TRAMWAYS. INTERESTING STATEMENT BY ALDERMAN ROBERTS.</p>
        <p>Further Extensions Decided Upon.</p>
        <p>An interesting statement was presented to the Warrington Town Council on Tuesday evening by Akerman Roberts (chairman of the Tramways Committee) with reference to the financial position of the Tramways undertaking. At this stage, we will give the minutes of tho Tramways Committee which were submitted for the confirmation of the Council.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Committee on the 9th April the manager was authorized to advertise for tenders tor uniforms for the car drivers and conductors. it was resolved that the sum of £345 be deposited with the London and North-Western Railway Com- pany to cover the estimated cost of signalling and safety appliances to be constructe lat the level crossing at Wilderepool Tenders for the overhead equipment and permanent way for tramways No 3 (pare of), 45, and 6, were opened, signed by the Chairman, and ordered to be forwarded to Messrs Preece and Cardew for report thereon. Messrs Preece and ardew having submitted their report on the tenders receive i for additional tram ways, it was resolved that the tender of Messre Bennie and Thompson for the construction of the permanent way for tramways No 3 (part of). 4,5, and 6, at £16.857 144 61 be accepted, subject to the sanction of the. Board of Trade to the borrowing of the money ; and that the tender of Messrs It W Black• well and Company, Limited, for the eonstruction of overhead eßlipment for tramways No 3 (part of), 4, 5 and 6 at £2,736 194 21 be accepted, subject to the sanction of the Board of Trade to the borrowing of the money. Messrs Preece and Cardew submitted the following estimate of the cost of constructing tramways No 3 (part of) 4,5, and 6:— Permanent way, £17,000; electrical equipment, £3,000; switchgear and feelers, £500: cars,£4,soo; en gi ri-eriug and contingeecies, £2,000 ; total, £27,000. It was resolved that applicati ,n be made to the Board of Trade for their sanction to the bor- rowing of the sum of £27,000 for the construction of the said tramways ; also, that the Borough Treasurer be auteorized to take out a policy with 1 the Ocean Accident and Guarantee Corporation, Limited, to cover the sum of £5OO for any one acci- dent, but not exceeding £2,000 in any one year on payment of 35s per cent per annum on the gross receipts. In moving the confirmation of the minutes of the committee the deputy-mayor said he might ex- plain that the resolution relating to the sum of £345 to be deposited with the London and North- Western Railway Company to cover the estimated cost of signalling and safety appliances to be con- structed at the level crossing at Wlllerspool was a second sum that bad been deposited. The first sum voted by the Council, on the recommendation of the committee, was for the alterations to the perman- ent way. The present suria was for the necessary signalling and safety appliances. Whatever differ- ence there was between the amount actually de- posited and the actual cost would be returned to the Council by the London and North-Western Rail- way Company. Then there were resolutions on the minutes accepting certain tenders, and there was a resolution giving notice of their intention to apply for power to borrow £27,000 on account of the tenders accepted, He thought the Council would be glad to see that the committee had,ae far as they considered desirable, taken the precaution of insur- ing themselves against liability for accidents to third parties as explained in the resolution. The amount, for which they were covered was sufficient, and he should be sorry, as chairman of the committee, it it should become necessary to call upon the insur- ance company for any serious portion of the amount. (Hear, hear.) In reference to the tenders which had been accepted, might he briefly refer to the capital expenditure on account of the tram- ways, and make it as clear as he could, and also evident to this ratepayers, what the original esti- mate was and what had lactaally been spent ? He wished to do so because there were some rather erroneous ideas flying about which it was desirable to correct. In the first place the total amount of the estimates submitted to the Board of Trade inspectors and approved by ttTrn was £102,831. That amount was male up in this was : Latchford to Sankey Bridges route. £4,400; Wilderspool, £17,650; Longford to the Ceme- tery, £27,081 : Cemetery to Padeate Bridge, £5,500; Cemetery to Bruche Bridge, £4200 Toe tenders submitted that evenin7 covered Longford Bridge to the Cemetery, and not from , the Cemetery to Psdeate Bridge or from the Cemetery to Brioche Bridge. The total amounts of the estimates to which the Council would be committed, if the resolu- tions submitted for confirmation that night were ap- proved, was £93,131. The actual expenditurs against that o,timate up to date was £88,251 4s Id. That sum did not include the cost of certain street alterations, which would not amount to very much, hoc whatever it circa to would have to go to the debit of that account Neither did it include on the credit side the value of any of the old material, setts. Sel , taken up, and displaced by new material out down by the Tramways Cemeittee He thought he might explain that that erpeoditure would net exceed the amount which he had named of £88.251 44 11 very much, and thv, e estitn .ta provided for that same work of £93 131 would be: found very ample indeed for the purposea for which it had been borrowed. The sum of £27,000 which they proposed to ask the Council to nermit them to apply for was the sum included in the £93,131 as the estimate for Longford Bridge to the Cemetery r,ute. Ho had personally suggested the po4aibility that the corn- mitt-e might be induced to carry their work from the Cemetery to Padgate Bridge provided they considered there was, a sufficient amount of traffic to jestify the additional expenditure of £5.500, but if that was decided upon the committee would c me to the Council again for their approvsl. In any case if it was decided upon it was already include,d in the original amount sub- mitted to the Beard of Irtide inspectors, and approved of hv them, and which amount for everything—was £lO2 831. On the same basis out of the estimate of £93,131, their estimates up to date, inclueing the figures they were suhm,tting that evening, for appro ea! i amounted to £86,251 4s 11. Sometimes t was (Ifficult to enter into a new kind of wbrk without over-stepping the estimate,' originally submitted, but the committee were in this hapoy position that, instead of exceeding their estimates,they were exceedingly well within them. (Hear. hear.) .The Alderman then proceeded to refer to the traffic recem's from the new cars, lee. For the week ending Sunday, April 27th, the traffic receipts were :</p>
        <p>Latchrrlrel. Sa-Ic,. Tni-11 Passengers 38840 19478 58318 Rsceipts £l6l 16 8 £Bl 3 2 £24.2 19 10 Car miles _ __ ___ 3306</p>
        <p>Receipts per car mile 18•900 15•569 17 640 Units per car mile... -- .980 Units consurwd ... 3243 These figures were, the Alderman said, for the first week, which they did not take in any Sense as their guide for the future. The COS were such a novelty that the people indulged in their use very freely, to the great satisfaction of the committee. The second week wai not so satisfactory as the first, but still it swim qoite satis- factory considering that the system bad onli just- been initiated. The figures were as follows fur the week ending Sunday, May 4th .. La'chferd. Sinirey. Tui-al. Passengers 30272 20001 50273 ' £B3 6 9 2001</p>
        <p>Recaipts 4011</p>
        <p>Cu Mile</p>
        <p>2010</p>
        <p>Receipts pr car mil 15 060 9 995 12 533 Unite per car mile — '939 Units consutiv‘cl — . . 3767 . ..</p>
        <p>Alderman Roberts said he thought they might be satisfied that their inauguration of the system had teen satisfKciory—(hear, hear)—and without in any sense holding out the idea that they were going to make a tremendous amount of money out of the man. agemect of toe tramways, he thought the committee were wise in declining to accept the proposals of an outside company ti take over the management of the tramways even upon the guiranteed basis they offered. (Hear, hear.) If an outside company thought they could mike it a profitable undertaking he thought it was tbe duty of the committee at any rate to try to make it a profitable undertaking themselves, and if they had what he thought they would have, the cordial I cb-operation and support of the townspeople, he hid no hesitation in saying that he believed that they would ultimately, pf.rhaps not immediately, make a satisfac- tory profit. (Hear, hear.) Ha did not at all anticipate anything in the shape of a financial loss of ens moment even at the end of the first financial year. (Hear, bear.) , Alderman MONKS srcouded the motion for the con- firmatian of the minutes. Councillor JOHN ROBERTS: Do the amounts include the Winwick;.street and Winwick-road route ? The DEPUTY Wawa : Everything. Councillor BENNETT said that in common with the rest of the members of the Couneil, be had listened to the statement which had been made by Alderman RMerts, with great pleasure. He had always had considerable faith in the tramways, and although he was not yet reconciled to the hideous iverhead equipment, In every other respect be was more than ever satisfied. Now he wished to say a few words about the possibility of an extensioa of the tramways to Padgate. As one who had Interests there he should like to put in a word for Oat</p>
        <p>end of the town. The committee would be well advised to extend the tramway to the bridge, as Padgate was an exceedingly desirable and growing locality and deserved these facilities. Be did not think that that end of the town tel had quite fair play, and he thought the time had come when that eligible suburb should receive a little more attention from the Tramways Committee and the Corporation generally. The population of Padgate and the surrounding district was sufficient to make it worth their a bile to extend the tramway line. The DEPCITY-MAXOR : If the Padgate people can show us that it will be to our advantage to do so we have the power. Our power lapses in August next. Councillor TINNiox : Would you run for a penny ? Fbe DEPUTY-MAYOR : That is a matter for coneidera- ton.</p>
        <p>Councillor BENNETT asked if the Parish Council would be the proper p:reon3 to move in the matter.</p>
        <p>The DEenTvl4lAvon : A substantially signal petition would be as effective.</p>
        <p>The minutes were then agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>An Outside Opinion.</head>
        <p>An Outside Opinion.</p>
        <p>" The Light Railway and Tramway Journal" for May 2ed contains an illustrated article on the Warring- ton Corporation Tramways. It says:—" The Warrington Corporation tramway system, althougn only of compara- tively small extent, being 3120 miles in length, ia still of considerable interest, and will serve a population of some 65,000 to 70,000 in an admirable manner. There are many large iron, wire and other manufactories in the district, and the artisan population will doubtless serve to make the system a financial success. The dis- trict is quite easy for tramway purposes, the steepest gradient being not more theu 1 in 27, and the smallest radius of any curve is 50tr. The system has been constructed in a comparatively short period, the track under the supervision of Mr Lengdin, borough surveyor, and the electrical equipment by Mr Grimsdale borough electrical engineer and tramways manager, under the supervision of Messrs Preece and Cardew as I the consulting engineera " The eyetma is described in great detail, and a description is also given of the power holds°. With reference to the last named, the journal remarks " Tnia is an excellent structure, with plenty of room and arrangements which are highly creditable to all concerned in the designing and carrying out of the work." Referring to the cundenaing system, the journal continues " Fur condensing purposes the river water is used, and in this connection it may be mentione I as a curious fact that certain boilers in the neighbourhood which are tcd from the river were recently declared to be dealer than any boilers in the locality, a fact, which, we think, may be Attributed to the numerous tanneries in the district which discharge their bp-products into the river." Coming to the car sheds, the wnter says "an ingenious and economical arrangement has been effected in respect of the car sheds. The Corporation are also owners of the gas works and having to construct new works,the old retort house has been adopted as the car shed for the tramways, and with the alterations which have been effected, it makes Cipital buildieg for the purpose. It will contain 26 cars and has 8 bays."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>CORONATION.</head>
        <p>THE CORONATION.</p>
        <p>St. Helens Festival.</p>
        <p>The S. Helens Coronation Proceedings Committee have succeeded in obtainisg the use of Grange Park for the great Coronation tete on Thursday, June 26. The Mayor will, it is bFlieved, present to all the infants attending the elementary schools commemorative medals, and to all toe children of the upp-r standards placques, similar Co those given by Colonel R Pilkington at the Diamond Jubilee. The managers of schools will be allowed 6t per head for the children, by means of which they may provide a meal on Coronation Day or some other day according to the convenience of arrange-. ments. The Vd.u,ve Ira and rwaio.:lrs of the Biys Brigade + will have is each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Earlestown and Newton</head>
        <p>Earlestown and Newton</p>
        <p>A meeting of the csinmittee which ha a charge of the arrangements in connection with the local celebration of the Coronation, was h !Id in the town Hall, Earles- town on Wednesday evening. Councillor W Collingwood presided. A letter was read from Mr J T Austin, secre- tary of the Free Caurch C stating that the non- conformist Sunday schools amid not accede to the invitation of the committoe,to take part in the proposed pracesaien, and it was decided to invite toe Day schools only. The following programme, subject to alteration, was agreed to.—Procession :—Section A Volunteers ; Viaduct Band ; Police Inspector Morgan, Criairman of District Council and Clerk ; Members of Council ; Officials of 161 ; Clergy and Ministers of all denominations ;—Section B. Trades and Friendly Societies in order of date of touudatimi.--dection C. Detachment of Fire Brigade with steam fire engine ; horse ambulance ; Salvation Army band ; farm school children ; clay school children ; police. It is expected that the farm ACII3OI 133 present. The muster will be at 11. o'clock on the Msrket ground and the procession will be by way of Cross-lane, Crow-lane, and high-street to the Meshes at Newton. There will also he a treat to Old Folks, the Committee to manage this to comprise the mmiaters, Mr W El Elindloy, and tlr T Unsworth, with power to add to their number. la the evening there will be a display of fireworks and each child will be presented with a mag ii comarrnoration of the event. Any trades or friendly cieties deciding to take part in the procession should communicate with Mr Colo, Clerk to the Council.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>LATE COUNCILLOR PLUMPTON. References at the Town Council Meeting.</head>
        <p>THE LATE COUNCILLOR PLUMPTON. References at the Town Council Meeting.</p>
        <p>At the monthly meeting of the Town Council on Tuesday evenisig the Mayor (Itlr S Ulearlten Parr) said the Council would receive with mirth regret the announcement of the death of a prominent and active memher of the Council, Councillor Piumpton, who was With tlet2l at their list meeting a mouth ago in appar- ently good health. Although his Acquaintance with the late councillor dated only a few manchs back, when he first had the honour of presiding over the Council, Coun- cillor Fluttapton wat, he believed, ths premier councillor, if he might so express it, of the birough. Tti9v all knew how assiduous and annstant he was in his attend- ance at the Council and committee meetings, and how business-like and straightforward he was in tho work he had to do, having the courage of his convictions and expressing them freety and fearlessly. He served the town honourably and conscientiously to the best of his ability and he (the Mayor) felt sure it would be the Council's wish, as it wan his, to express thf ir condolende to his family on the loss they had sustained. He moved a resolution that the Council received with deep regret the announcement of Councillor Plurnpton's death, and desired to record the loss it had sustained by his death, and requested the Town Clerk to convey to the family the sine re sym- pathy a the Council with them in their bereavement. The DEPUTY OSAYOR (Alderman Roberts)seeondediand said he entirely endorsed what the Mayor bad so fittingly and eloquently said. Alderman 'SUTTON supported. He said the late Cauncillor was a member of two committees (the Health Committee and the Street Improvement Committee) of which he (Alderman Sutton) was chairman, and no member ever tried to fulfil the duties connected with 1 the committees more than he did. He felt sore his death would be a groat 1093 to the town at large. Councillor SMETHURST, said, as chairman of the Fire Engine Committee, of whiea deceased was v ice-Jaair- I man, he could endorse all that had been said.</p>
        <p>Councillor JAMES EVANS, Ai vpresontative of the ward t,.) which the late Councillor belonvd, said it had been his pleasure to know Mr Plumptoo ever since he entered the Council and I efore that date. He was always sincere conscientio Is and energetic in what ne undertook, and the sp-aker endorsed the sentiments that had alnair heen f'XpreSSe . . -</p>
        <p>- ri rpsolutbn was thee put by the :Mayon, and carried,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Paving and Sewerage:Committee</head>
        <p>The Paving and Sewerage:Committee</p>
        <p>and the Deceased.</p>
        <p>The Paving and Sewerage C immittee held their fort- nightly meeting on Monday evening. Alderman Heaton presided,and thero were also preseni- Alderman Hutchin- son, C.-iunclllors Tieion, Smethur,t, Davies Bin], Burton, Rogers, and A Jolley, Mr Lingdin (b-irough surveyor). and Mr Wilson (deputy to wt. clerk). Previ- ous to the cimmencement of business, the Chairman reminded toe cimmittee that tiley were one meminer short—the late Councillor Plurnpton• It was °nig fitting that be should refer to him that eveiaing,for he was sure they all felt his loss, for he was most zealous in the dis- charge of his duties as a member of that committee, and of the interests of toe ward he represented. He moved th It an expression of their deep regret and cons dolence be forwarded to the family of the deceased. Councillor SMETTIOR4T, i-, seconding the motion, said he always found du fate Clunciihr Pluinprion a in St straiehtferward and conscientious tn in, always most earnest in looking after the welfare of the ward he repre- sented. Hi Wag afraid the Waite Cross Ward would have a difficulty in finding another representativ,l like him. _</p>
        <p>me Motion was carried unanimodsly, and the Town Clerk Was inrructed to forward a copy of it to the deceased'a family. Councillor JOHN ROBERTS was unanimously el'cted a member of the c,,mmir"e•</p>
        <p>•A Sr. HELENS WORKM&amp;N 81TOCICINGLY BURNED.—Jitnee Stevens, aged thir-v-nin. of 20, Natcrove_roAd, admitted to the St Thiene Hospital on WednesdavOcffor- ing from ext.nsive burns. Stevens was working in the boilerbouse at Lea Green Colliery, when part of the fire- place came out, and he was struck by the beat and flames and burned all over the face, neck,arms, and lege. He was attended by Dr Jackson, and taken to the hoapi- tal in the new horse ambulance, which has been recently purchased by the committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1902-05-09_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>EDUCATION BILL.</head>
        <p>THE EDUCATION BILL.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Corporation Bill Dropped.</p>
        <p>The Parliamentary agents of the Warrington Corpora: tion (Messrs 3aker, Lees and Co.) haying been informed that the Government wonld oppose the turther progress of the Warrington Corporation (Education) Bill, under which it was proposed to make the Corporation the educational authority for the borough, endowing them with all the powers of school board, the promoters of the Bill have abandoned the measure. Of course, if the Government's Education Bill passes this session the Warrington Corporation will have just the same powers as they sought under their private Bill, Wa.rington Iring a county borough, though they will at the same time be saddled wirh the whole of the cost of elemen- tary educatien ia the borough except that part which is provided for out of the public funds. General Declaration of the Executive</p>
        <p>Committee of the Warrington and District Evangelical Free Church Council.</p>
        <p>The following statement has been issurd by the War- rington Free Church Council. It should nave appeared last week but, through an official omission, reached us too late for publication. We have long felt that a system of education which should be co-ordinate, representative, and national, was greatly-needed, and we had hoped that the Bill promised by the Government would meet the requirements of the case. Our hope, however, has not been realised. After carefully considering the Government measure,we have been forced to the conclusion that it will contribute more to the interests of Ecclesiasticism than of E iucation.</p>
        <p>The Bill pr -poses to impose the whole cost of equip- ment and maintenance ot Denominational Schools upon the rates and taxes, and at the same time to allow the Control to remain in the hands of persons not responsible to the public. There are 8,000 parishes in England in which the only elementary schoo's are Anglican. These schools for all practical purposes are under the control of the parish Clergyman, but the children of Free Church parents are necessarily compelled to attend them and the religious instruction given in them is Sacerdotal in character and tendency. In such schools Noncon- formist children are frequently taught that Dissent is a sin, that their parents are schismatics, ad that belief in certain doctrines, which Evangelical Free Churchmen regard as erronroas, is essential to salvation. Unfor- tunately the Conscience Clause is practically useless, because many of the parents dare not claim its protec- tion, and it is not fair to the children that they should be subjected to invidious distinctions. Moreover many inducements are held out for the purpose of detaching children from the Churohes of their parents and gather- ing them into the Anglican fold.</p>
        <p>The Bill introduced by Mr itilfour proposes appar- ently to safegusrd the interests of the Free Churchmen in villages by permitting, under certain conditions, the erection of additional denominational schools, robe sup- ported by Government grants and money taken Ism the rates. This part of the Bill is,in our opinion worth- less for the purpese for which it is intended, and would be even mischievous by increasing the number of small ind inefficient schools, when one school is all a district requires. Our leading objections to the Bill may be summarised as follows :</p>
        <p>(1) It is designed to pay for Denominational teaching with ratepayers' money, with,Jut giving the ratepayers *ny real or effective ccntrol. thus violating one of the fundamental principles of good government, namely, that where there is taxation there ought to be representation. (2) That it provides for tie increase of Denomina- tional schools, the maintenance of which must be borne by public funds- (3) That while professing to allay the contentions of creeds and parties, it furnishes fresh poasibili- ties for Denominational rivalry and bitterness, and would crtainly introduce sectarian strife into municipal affairs. (4) That it proposes to make County Councils, Dis- trict Councils, and County Borough Councils the Educational Authorities, and thus to trans- fer power from Bards specially appointed to perform educational duties, to Councils already overburdened with other and very different work. (5 That it makes no attempt to improve the provi- sion made for the training of teachfrl, but still leavcs thia to Denominational Colleges which impose r,ligims tests and give s ctarian bias. (6) That although half the scholars and teachers in e'ementary schools are females, it excludes women from direct appointment to the manage- ment, inasmuch as women are not eligible for County or Borough Councils. (7) That nearly half of the 143,370 persons employed as teachers of various grades in the elementary schools will continue t bs subject to theologi- cal tests as a condition of employment,although their salaries will be paid entirely from public funds. (8) That the provisions made for Secondary Educa- tion are crude and inadequate, and likely to produce perplexity and trouble. _</p>
        <p>In thus stating our objsct ons to the E lunation Sill we are fully aware of the difficulties in the way of a satisfactory settlement of the Education question, and have no wish to be captious or unreasonaele, We will heartily welcarne any measure which offers a fair and Pat settlement. and will gladly render all the help within our power to further the same. Belttving, as we do, that the Bill now before the House of Commons would interfere with Religious Free- dom, prevent popular control of schools supported from public funds, Inv 'lye the zonntry in enormous expense, load the ratepayers with heavy burdens and retard the Educational progress of the country, we feel it to be our duty to resist it by all constitutional means, and in this endeavour we seek the co-operation of all Warrington Free Churchmen. The strugslsi in which we are thus engaged is not of our seekinz. It has been forced upou us, and we dare not shirk it We would be unworthy of our traditions if we allowed an injustice to to, done to us and to °dr children, without making an effort to Prevent it. We /sone that the Free Churchmen of War- rington will rally at the call of duty, and do all within their power to ?prevent the passing of this Education Bill as it now stands before the country. (Signed) CHARLES E, PARKER, President. 3013 N YONGE, E. T. REED Vice-Presidents. C. H. HOCKEN, R. PIERCE WILLIAMS.I- Secretaries.</p>
        <p>The Wesleyan Heresy base —Principal Agar</p>
        <p>Beet's. Position.</p>
        <p>" The Christian CoMenonwealth " states that Pro- fessor Beet, D. o.,principal of Richmond alinisterial Training College, is to be removed from that posi- tion by the General Committee which governs the four Wesleyan Colleges It is !indorse° yl that Dr. Beet's off glee is that he 'loss not assert the endless torment ot the lot. Ile dues not set up any epp.)s- ing doctrine, but simply says that the Bible does not definitely state what is the ultimare fete of the unsaved. The " Christian World ' publishes an account of an interview with Professor Beet, in which he is reported to have said : "I hold that the Bible gives no warrant for saying either that the lost are condemned to eternal suffering or that they are annihilated. I know that they are ruined,' that is all. I object to the traeliti;eial doctrine and to the view of Sd- ward White as going beyond the statements of the Bible. I may say that Bishop Gore has accepted my view in his' Commentary on Romans," He re- sta'es it in his own words and mentions me by name Another thing : I have given proof of my good faith in imperilling my position by teaching what, I believe. Whether I am right or wrong. I have had the courage of my convic- tions. and lam prepared to stand by them. They are rho re:ult of twenty-five years study. I feel that I have done right, for believe that God has taught me something about the future. lam quite certain it would have h,sen dishonest to conceal my opinions." Dr Bet will long be remembered as labouring acclotably in the Warrington Wesleyan circuit</p>
