        <p>.Atord .Ribblesdale and Mr. Wertheimer. Mr. Shannon stands only second to the great Anglo- ' American master, and his Lady Marjorie Manners 111 certainly one of the pictures of the year. The popular " 8.-P." is portrayed by Mr. G. F. Watts, and there are other most notable pictures too numerous to mention, of which the President, Sir Edward Poynter, contributes Storm Nymphs, which is very prettily drawn, and Sir Laurence Alma Tadema a tiny masterpiece in Caracalla. In con- trast, there is plenty- of inferior work, as un. fortunately is only too usual in the Royal Academy. Policeman Jones, of Leeds, is attracting a good deal of attention with No. 347, which is a highly creditable piece of landscape. As a rule golf is a game in which the non- ptaying public take comparatively little interest, though, once an iron has been handled, one immediately becomes an enthusiast, and .thence- forward every other branch of sport usually occupies a second place. The Championship Meeting which has just been brought to a close on the Royal Liverpool Club's links at Hoylake, was somewhat of an exception in the matter of the</p>
