        <p>THE AMATEUR GOLF CHAMPIONSHIP. Won by a Warringtonian. The result of the final tie in the Amateur Golf Championship at floylake on Friday, has resulted in 'groat honour being done Warrington, for the winner is Mr. Charles Hutonings, a native of the good old town, and formerly engaged in tanning at Howley in the firm of J. and W. N. Hutchings. His opponent in the flea' was Mr. S. H. Fry, of the Mid-Surrey Club. and better known to the world as the ex- smateur billiard:champion. On the outward journey Mr. Hutchings played brilli- antly and had a eubstantial lead, but subsequently Mr. Fry played a magnificent uphill game, and the basal man was left a winner by the narrow margin of one hole. a The victory of the home player was greeted with a storm of applause. A number of golfers 'immediately seized the champion and carried him shoulder high to the clubhouse. Fry walked to the clubhouse and was enthusiastically cheered for his plucky fight. Subs ,quently the cup was presented to the winner by Mr A G Rankine, captain of toe R -rya Liverpool Club. Mr Charles Elutohinge, wile resides at Hoylake is an old member of the Royal Liverpool Club. He started golf rather comparatively late in life, at the age of thirty, and has no eeen fifty—three summers. He has earned toe distinction of being the only golfer who with anything like his years has woo the amateur chlunPienshiP. Naturally his victory is very popular, not only among his own club mates, but also among golfers generally. He was formerly in business at a large tannery works at Warrington, but retired some years ago ind as the Captain humorously observed in banding the cup, is the only grandfather who has won the cup. We were apprised of the gratifying result of the championship tie by Mr A El Crosfield, who sent us a wire commencing with the joyous exclamation. 'WARRINGTON TO THE FORE." Mr Crosfield himself was one of the competitors, but euccuinted to the prowess of an ex-champion. Mr John Ball. jun., in the first round, by seven up ani six to play. FIREWORKS CELEBR A.TIONS AT W ARRINGTO N. Mr Hutchinga' victory was celebrated in Warrington the same night by a pyrotechniefdisplay on the Arpley Cricket . Ground, Messrs James Fairclough and A H Crosfield having both telegraphed requesting that' this should be dose, The fireworks were suppliael by Mr Parkinson, who discharged them between 8.30' and 9 O'clock. e night being clear they attracted a great deal of attention. FLORILINE 1--No - THE TEETH AND BREATH. -ThOrrarighy cleanses the teeth (row all parasites or impurltlee, harden the gums, prevt.nte \tartar, stops decay, and gives to the teeth a peculiar pearly whiteness and a delightful fragrance to the breath. Price Es. 6d. for the liquid; or is. per jar for the Florian* ",Powder." of all Chemists-and Perfumer..— .4Elft VALTYABLE DISZOVEHY NOR THE your hair is taming grey or white or falling off use the MEXICAN HAIR Emma% for it will positively restore, in every case, grey or white hair to its original colour. It makes the hair charmingly beautiful, as we las promoting the growth. Price 3e 6d per bottle.— Arta. To 310THERa.—gra. Wlnelow's Soothing Syrup has been used for over fifty years by millions of mothers for their Andrea while teething, with perteot success._ It will relieve the roorenfferer Immediately It is pleasant to taste ; and produoes natural (inlet sleep, oy relieving the child from pain and the tittle eherab awakes "as bright as a button." Of all Ohetniata Is led per bottle. .Advt. OLARKB'S B 41 PILLS are warranted to cure' in either sex, all acquired or constitutional Discharges from the Urinary Organs, Gravel and Pains in the baok. Free from Mercury. Established upwards of 30 yems. In boxes 4s. 6d. each, of all Chemists and Patent Medicine Vendor. through- out the world, or sent for slaty stamps by the makers. The Lincoln and MidlandACounties Drugeompany. Achrt.</p>
